window.COURSE_INDEX={"p":[["electrostatics/","Chapter 0","Electrostatics","Electrostatics for electrochemistry: charge, field, potential and current","A torch cell says 1.5 V on its side. What is a volt, and what is being pushed?"],["galvanic-cells/","Chapter 1","Galvanic cells","The galvanic cell: half-reactions, open-circuit voltage and two modes of conduction","Why does the lamp light when the switch closes, and not while it is open?"],["cell-anatomy/","Chapter 2","Cell anatomy","Anatomy of a lithium-ion cell: components, cell geometry and the rocking-chair principle","If we cut open the battery in a phone, what would we find?"],["cell-voltage/","Chapter 3","Cell voltage","Cell voltage: electrode potentials, the electrical double layer and the electrolyte stability window","Why does a lithium-ion cell give over 3.6 V and a torch cell 1.5 V?"],["capacity-energy/","Chapter 4","Capacity and energy","Capacity, energy and power: Faraday's law, the C-rate and the Ragone plot","What do mAh, Wh and Wh/kg mean, and how long would your cell run a lamp?"],["polarization/","Chapter 5","Polarization","Galvanostatic cycling and polarization: overpotentials, hysteresis and heat generation","What happens when we charge a cell and use it, and why does it get warm?"],["voltage-profiles/","Chapter 6","Voltage profiles","The voltage profile: faradaic and capacitive storage, phase equilibria and many-particle electrodes","Why is the curve of LiFePO₄ flat while that of LiCoO₂ slopes?"],["insertion-hosts/","Chapter 7","Insertion hosts","Insertion hosts: intercalation, alloying and conversion reactions","When a cell is charged, where is the lithium stored?"],["liquid-electrolytes/","Chapter 8","Liquid electrolytes","Liquid electrolytes: formulation, salt dissociation and Li⁺ solvation","What is the liquid inside, and why these molecules?"],["ion-transport/","Chapter 9","Ion transport","Ion transport and the electrode–electrolyte interface: diffusion, migration and transference","How does an ion cross the cell, and what does it meet at the electrode?"],["sei/","Chapter 10","The SEI","The solid electrolyte interphase (SEI): formation, passivation and growth","What is the solid electrolyte interphase, and why does every cell need one?"],["composite-electrodes/","Chapter 11","Composite electrodes","Composite electrodes: percolation, porosity, tortuosity and transport limitations","Why is an electrode a porous coating of particles, carbon and binder?"],["negative-electrodes/","Chapter 12","Negative electrodes","Negative electrode materials: graphite, silicon, lithium metal and anode-free cells","What could replace graphite, and why has it not happened yet?"],["positive-electrodes/","Chapter 13","Positive electrodes","Positive electrode materials: layered oxides, spinels and olivine phosphates","Why are today's cells built on NMC or LFP, and what comes next?"],["solid-state/","Chapter 14","Solid-state cells","Solid electrolytes and solid-state cells: polymer and inorganic ion conductors","Why replace the liquid with a solid, and why is it so hard?"],["sodium-organic/","Chapter 15","Sodium and organic","Sodium-ion and organic electrode materials","Can sodium, or molecules made of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen, do lithium's job?"],["degradation/","Chapter 16","Degradation","Degradation: loss of lithium inventory, loss of active material and resistance rise","Why does my battery hold less after a few years?"],["safety/","Chapter 17","Safety","Safety: thermal runaway, protection strategies and the operating window","Can a battery catch fire, and how do we keep it safe?"],["recycling/","Chapter 18","Recycling","End of life: recycling of lithium-ion batteries","What happens to a battery when it is finished?"],["lab/cell-testing/","Lab 1","Cell testing","Cell assembly and galvanostatic testing: coin cells, cycling protocols and differential analysis","How is a test cell made, and what does a cycling test tell us?"],["lab/gitt/","Lab 2","GITT","Pulse methods and the galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT)","How do we measure resistance and diffusion?"],["lab/eis-measurement/","Lab 3","EIS: measurement","Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) I: principles, measurement and validation","How do we record an impedance spectrum we can trust?"],["lab/eis-analysis/","Lab 4","EIS: analysis","Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) II: equivalent circuits and the distribution of relaxation times","What does a spectrum mean, and what does a good fit not prove?"],["lab/post-mortem/","Lab 5","Post-mortem","Post-mortem and electrode characterization","When the outside of a cell is not enough, what then?"]],"e":[[0,"p","","","A torch cell says 1.5 V on its side. What is a volt, and what is being pushed?"],[0,"p","","","Where we are. Before the first cell, four words that every later screen uses: charge, the thing that pushes and is pushed; field, the push per unit charge at a place; potential, the energy per unit charge at a place; voltage, a difference of potential. Each gets one figure, one equation and one number."],[0,"f","f0-1","Figure 0.1","Coulomb’s law. Two charges of one elementary charge each, in vacuum. Try this: set r to 1 nm, read the force, then set 2 nm and check that it is a quarter; then flip one sign."],[0,"p","f0-1","figure 0.1","Everything on this page moves because charges push on one another. Charge comes in two signs, and the names are a convention older than the electron: Benjamin Franklin called an excess of what we now call electronic charge negative and a deficiency of it positive."],[0,"p","f0-1","figure 0.1","Between two charges q and q′ a distance r apart there is a force F = qq′/(4πε0r2), Coulomb’s law, where ε0 is a measured constant, the permittivity of free space. Read the formula: the force grows with either charge, falls with the square of the distance, and its sign follows the product of the charges, so like charges push apart and unlike charges pull together."],[0,"p","f0-1","figure 0.1","The unit of charge is the coulomb, and one electron carries 1.602 × 10−19 C of it. Put two electrons one nanometre apart, about the width of three water molecules, and the formula gives 0.23 nN pushing them apart: tiny in newtons, enormous for something that weighs 10−30 kg."],[0,"p","f0-1","figure 0.1","Charges push on each other with a force that falls as the square of the distance."],[0,"f","f0-2","Figure 0.2","Field and potential around a single charge. The map on the left and the graph on the right describe the same push in two languages. Try this: slide the probe from 1 nm to 4 nm and watch the force arrow shrink four times faster than the potential falls."],[0,"p","f0-2","figure 0.2","Take the second charge away and ask what is left at its place. The answer is the electric field: the force that would act on a unit charge put there, a vector with a direction and a strength at every point in space. Around a positive charge it points outward and falls as 1/r2, because that is how the force falls."],[0,"p","f0-2","figure 0.2","Now bring a unit positive charge in from very far away, pushing against that field all the way. The work you do is the electric potential φ at the point you reach, and it does not depend on the path you took: φ = −∫E·dl."],[0,"p","f0-2","figure 0.2","Only differences of φ between two points can be measured; the value at one point is a bookkeeping choice, and the usual choice sets φ = 0 infinitely far away. For a single elementary charge the integral gives φ = q/(4πε0r), which is 1.44 V one nanometre out and half that at two."],[0,"p","f0-2","figure 0.2","The two pictures are one: the field is the slope of the potential. Where φ falls steeply the push is strong; where φ is flat there is no push at all. That last case is about to matter."],[0,"p","f0-2","figure 0.2","Field is force per unit charge; potential is energy per unit charge; the field is the slope of the potential."],[0,"f","f0-3","Figure 0.3","Voltage as a potential hill. Two conductors, a gap, and a charge that rolls. Try this: flip the test charge to −e, press Play, and watch it climb toward the higher potential; then double V and watch the field arrows."],[0,"p","f0-3","figure 0.3","A voltage is a difference of potential between two points, and its unit, the volt, is one joule per coulomb: the energy available to move each coulomb of charge from one point to the other. Move a charge q across a potential difference Δφ and its energy changes by qΔφ."],[0,"p","f0-3","figure 0.3","For one electron across one volt that change is one electron-volt, and per mole of electrons it is 96.5 kJ, comparable to the energies of chemical bonds and reactions; which is why a few volts are enough to drive chemistry."],[0,"p","f0-3","figure 0.3","Metals are conductors: they contain charges that are free to move. Leave a metal alone and those charges settle until there is no field left inside it, because any field would move them further; so a conductor at rest sits at a single potential throughout, and any excess charge it carries lies on its surface."],[0,"p","f0-3","figure 0.3","The plates in the figure are two such flat regions of the potential; between them, where there is no conductor, the potential falls in a straight line and the field is simply V divided by the gap."],[0,"p","f0-3","figure 0.3","A picture to keep: potential is height, field is slope, and a positive charge rolls downhill while a negative one is pushed uphill. The picture breaks in one place: charges also push on each other and rearrange, so the hill is not fixed scenery. Chapter 3 is about how a cell builds its own hill."],[0,"p","f0-3","figure 0.3","A volt is a joule per coulomb; a conductor at rest is all at one potential."],[0,"f","f0-4","Figure 0.4","Why a current needs a voltage. The strip unrolls the whole loop, starting and ending at the − terminal. Try this: open the switch and watch where the drop goes; then close it and raise R until the lamp is barely lit."],[0,"p","f0-4","figure 0.4","A cell keeps its two terminals at a fixed potential difference; chapter 1 shows how. Join them with a wire and electrons flow through the wire from the more negative terminal to the more positive one. The amount of charge passing a point each second is the current, i = dQ/dt, measured in amperes: one ampere is one coulomb per second."],[0,"p","f0-4","figure 0.4","A wire carrying current is no longer at one potential. Between any two points on it there must be a potential difference to drive the current through the resistance between them. That is Ohm’s law, V = IR: the same current through twice the resistance needs twice the voltage."],[0,"p","f0-4","figure 0.4","The resistance of a piece of conductor grows with its length and shrinks with its cross-section, R = ρl/A, where ρ is the resistivity of the material. Metals have a resistance too, but it is so much smaller than that of an electrolyte that on the scale of a cell the drop along the copper is invisible. In the figure, nearly the whole voltage is spent across the lamp."],[0,"p","f0-4","figure 0.4","Energy per coulomb times coulombs per second is energy per second: the power delivered is P = IV, in watts."],[0,"p","f0-4","figure 0.4","Current needs a potential difference to drive it through resistance; the power is current times voltage."],[0,"f","f0-5","Figure 0.5","How an ion moves through a liquid. Our ion, the blue cation, will be followed by name through the rest of the page. Try this: double the ion radius and watch the drift halve; double the field and watch it double. Step through the launch to see the drag arrow grow until it matches the push."],[0,"p","f0-5","figure 0.5","In a liquid the mobile charges are not electrons but ions, atoms or molecules that have lost or gained electrons; free electrons in a solution are so short-lived that they carry no current worth counting. Put an ion of charge number z in a field E and it feels the force |z|eE. As it speeds up the liquid drags on it, with a drag that grows with speed; when the drag balances the push the ion stops accelerating and drifts at a steady terminal velocity. The velocity per unit field is the ion’s mobility, u = |z|e/(6πηr): a smaller ion in a thinner liquid moves faster."],[0,"p","f0-5","figure 0.5","When current flows through a liquid the potential slopes across it, exactly as it did along the wire, and the slope is the field: cations are driven down the slope and anions up it. Both carry current, and the liquid’s conductivity adds up every kind of ion, its charge, its mobility and its concentration: κ = FΣ|z|uC."],[0,"p","f0-5","figure 0.5","One more rule holds everywhere in the liquid: on any scale you can see, positive and negative charge balance. An imbalance cannot build up, because it would create an enormous field that would move ions until it was gone."],[0,"p","f0-5","figure 0.5","The constant F in that formula is the Faraday constant, the charge carried by one mole of electrons, 96 485.3 C. It is the bridge between the chemist’s moles and the physicist’s coulombs, and it returns in chapter 4 to count how much charge a gram of material can hold."],[0,"p","f0-5","figure 0.5","You can now explain what a volt is, why a field makes charges move and which way, why a current needs a potential difference to drive it, and why an ion in a liquid drifts at a steady speed rather than accelerating."],[0,"p","f0-5","figure 0.5","Now put two different metals into a liquid and see where a potential difference comes from."],[0,"p","f0-5","figure 0.5","Draw an imaginary closed surface anywhere. Gauss’s law says that the net charge inside it equals ε0 times the integral of the field over that surface, q = ε0∮E·dS. Inside a conductor at rest the field is zero everywhere, so the integral over any surface drawn inside the conductor is zero, so the charge inside is zero: whatever excess charge the conductor carries must lie on its boundary. In an electrolyte the same argument leaves the charge at the boundary too, but thermal motion smears it into a layer that can be tenths of a nanometre to thousands of nanometres thick, the space charge region; in a metal that layer is negligibly thin. Chapter 3 uses this to build the double layer at an electrode."],[0,"p","f0-5","figure 0.5","Bring an electrolyte next to the metal and the coulombic field of the metal’s surface charge is largely cancelled by the polarization of the liquid: the same mercury drop then needs about 10−6 C, some 6 × 1012 electrons, for a one-volt change, more than 107 times what it needed in vacuum."],[1,"p","","","Why does the lamp light when the switch closes, and not while it is open?"],[1,"p","","","Where we are. Chapter 0 gave you the volt (a joule per coulomb), the field (the push per unit charge), the conductor at rest (one potential throughout) and the two kinds of carrier: electrons in metals, ions in liquids. Now two different metals go into a liquid, and a potential difference appears between them."],[1,"f","f1-1","Figure 1.1","The Daniell cell, the textbook battery. Two metals, two salt solutions, a porous wall, and one wire. Try this: close the switch and watch the first zinc atom leave; then open it and notice that the voltmeter still reads 1.10 V."],[1,"p","f1-1","figure 1.1","Close the switch. Zinc atoms at the left electrode give up two electrons each and dissolve into the liquid as Zn2+ ions; at the right electrode Cu2+ ions take two electrons each and settle as copper metal. Each of those is a half-reaction, and neither can happen alone: each must be coupled, through a cell, to a second half-reaction running the other way."],[1,"p","f1-1","figure 1.1","The electrons cannot cross the liquid, so they take the wire, from the more negative electrode to the more positive, and the lamp lights on the way. Inside the cell the current is carried by ions: cations drift toward the copper and sulfate toward the zinc, so no charge piles up anywhere."],[1,"p","f1-1","figure 1.1","Open the switch and everything stops, but nothing is lost. The voltage you measure with nothing else connected is the open-circuit voltage, and a high-impedance voltmeter can read it without drawing any appreciable current. For this pair of metals it is 1.10 V: the difference between the standard potentials of the two half-reactions, +0.340 V for copper and −0.763 V for zinc."],[1,"p","f1-1","figure 1.1","Where a potential sits inside the cell is the strip under the figure: it jumps at each metal|liquid boundary, in a zone so narrow that a very strong field exists there, and stays flat through the liquid; the voltmeter reads the sum of the jumps along the path from one electrode to the other."],[1,"p","f1-1","figure 1.1","Chapter 3 shows how each jump is built: the chemistry moves a minute charge across the boundary, and the field of that separated charge is what the strip shows."],[1,"p","f1-1","figure 1.1","That is the whole trick, in Goodenough and Park’s words: the electrolyte “conducts the ionic component of the chemical reaction between the anode and the cathode, but it forces the electronic component to traverse an external circuit where it does work”."],[1,"p","f1-1","figure 1.1","A battery is a chemical reaction that is only allowed to happen through a wire."],[1,"f","f1-2","Figure 1.2","Two roads for charge, joined at the boundary. Why the electrons take the wire and the ions take the liquid. Try this: press Step and watch what happens at the boundary each time an ion arrives; nothing crosses without a reaction."],[1,"p","f1-2","figure 1.2","Why do the electrons choose the wire? Because they have no other road. In a metal, charge is carried by electrons moving through a lattice of fixed ion cores; in an electrolyte it is carried by ions, and the “free” electrons a liquid might briefly hold are so short-lived that they contribute nothing to its conductivity."],[1,"p","f1-2","figure 1.2","At the boundary between the two, charge can pass only if the two kinds of carrier are linked by an electrode reaction: electrons consumed or produced in the metal, ions produced or consumed in the liquid."],[1,"p","f1-2","figure 1.2","The field does the rest. One field points from the positive side to the negative side; it pushes a positive ion along itself and an electron against itself, so the cations in the liquid and the electrons in the wire go round the circuit in opposite senses and meet again at the electrodes."],[1,"p","f1-2","figure 1.2","In the wire, the potential difference that drives the electrons through the lamp is the one you met in figure 0.4; in the liquid, the potential slopes gently and drives the ions."],[1,"p","f1-2","figure 1.2","Electrons travel in metals, ions travel in liquids, and only a reaction can hand charge from one to the other."],[1,"f","f1-3","Figure 1.3","Cells and batteries. Voltage adds along a path; charge adds across paths. Try this: switch to series and count the steps on the strip; then to parallel and ask yourself which cell a given coulomb came from."],[1,"p","f1-3","figure 1.3","Strictly, one of these units is a cell, and several connected together are a battery, although the two words are used loosely. Connecting cells in series adds their voltages; connecting them in parallel adds their current and their run time. The strip says why the voltages add: a potential difference between two points is the sum of the rises and drops along any path between them, so three cells in a chain give three rises of V, and a coulomb that travels the whole chain picks up energy at each one."],[1,"p","f1-3","figure 1.3","Why the capacity does not add in series is a matter of counting coulombs. The cells in a chain carry one and the same current: the charge that leaves the first cell has nowhere to go but through the second and the third, so every coulomb drawn from the chain is a coulomb drawn from each cell. When one cell has given up its Q, they all have. In parallel the picture is reversed. All three + terminals are tied together and all three − terminals are tied together, so the ends of the battery see one potential difference, V, and no more; but the current is shared, each cell supplies a third of it, and together they can deliver three times the charge before they are empty. Series adds the rises along the path; parallel adds the paths."],[1,"p","f1-3","figure 1.3","A battery keeps its reacting materials inside, so it is a closed system: storage and conversion happen in the same box. A fuel cell is an open system, fed with fuel and oxidant from outside, so its energy is stored in a tank and converted in the cell. A supercapacitor stores charge in the electrical double layers at its electrode surfaces, without a chemical reaction."],[1,"p","f1-3","figure 1.3","None of the three turns heat into work, so none is bound by the Carnot limit that caps every heat engine. Everything on this page happens inside a single cell."],[1,"p","f1-3","figure 1.3","You can now explain why a cell has a voltage even when nothing is connected, where inside the cell that voltage sits, why the electrons take the wire and the ions the liquid, and why voltages add in series."],[1,"p","f1-3","figure 1.3","So what is inside the box, part by part, and what does each part do?"],[1,"p","f1-3","figure 1.3","A primary battery is assembled in the charged state and used until exhausted. A secondary, or rechargeable, battery can be restored to its charged condition by a current flowing in the opposite direction to the discharge current; since secondary batteries are usually assembled in the discharged state, they must be charged before their first use. To charge, a power supply is connected across the cell and its voltage is adjusted; the interfacial potential differences shift, especially at the electrode|electrolyte boundaries, and current can be driven in either direction. Which reactions can be run backwards, and which cannot, is a question for the modules still to come."],[2,"p","","","If we cut open the battery in a phone, what would we find?"],[2,"p","","","Where we are. Chapter 1 showed the four jobs in a cell: give electrons, take electrons, carry ions, keep the two electrodes apart. Now the parts that do those jobs in a real cell, and why they have the shapes they have."],[2,"f","f2-1","Figure 2.1","A coin cell, the kind used to test new materials in research laboratories, cut through the middle and pulled apart layer by layer. Try this: hover or tap each layer to read its job; drag the slider to close the stack; then find the two layers that decide the voltage."],[2,"p","f2-1","figure 2.1","Every cell has the same four jobs. The negative electrode gives up electrons: it is a good reducing agent, such as lithium, zinc or lead. The positive electrode takes them: an electron acceptor, such as lithium cobalt oxide, manganese dioxide or lead oxide. The electrolyte “provides pure ionic conductivity” between the two: it lets ions through and blocks electrons."],[2,"p","f2-1","figure 2.1","The separator is a porous barrier, permeable to ions and inert in the cell, that keeps the electrodes from touching. If they touched, the whole stored energy would be released as heat inside the cell."],[2,"p","f2-1","figure 2.1","Anode or cathode? You will meet both words, and they name a reaction, not a piece of metal: the anode is the electrode where oxidation releases electrons into the external circuit, the cathode the electrode where reduction takes them back. On discharge the negative electrode is oxidized and the positive electrode is reduced, so the negative electrode is the anode and the positive electrode the cathode; that is the battery convention. On charge the charger drives the current the other way: electrons are pushed into the negative electrode, which is now reduced, and pulled out of the positive electrode, which is now oxidized, so by the reaction definition the names swap, and the graphite that was the anode while you used the phone is the cathode while it charges. The metals stay where they are and keep their signs; only the labels move. To avoid the confusion this page says negative electrode and positive electrode from here on."],[2,"p","f2-1","figure 2.1","In the coin cell above, graphite sits on copper foil and the lithium host on aluminium, exactly as in the first commercial lithium-ion cell. Each foil is a current collector: a metal path for the electrons between the coating and the terminal. The electrolyte is a liquid, added drop by drop during assembly: in Murray, Hall and Dahn’s research cells, about 38 mg of a 1 mol/L solution of LiPF6 in a carbonate solvent, enough to soak the separator and wet both electrodes."],[2,"p","f2-1","figure 2.1","New positive-electrode materials are usually tested in a “half cell” against a piece of lithium foil; such cells are easy to make but can fail to predict how the material behaves against graphite in a full cell, which is why full coin cells like this one are built."],[2,"p","f2-1","figure 2.1","Every part has one job, and the electrolyte’s job is to let ions through and block electrons."],[2,"f","f2-2","Figure 2.2","Why cells are built from thin, wide layers, and how the layers fit in a can. Try this: drag the slider slowly and watch the five layers keep their order as they curl."],[2,"p","f2-2","figure 2.2","Ions move through an electrolyte far more slowly than electrons move through a metal (chapter 0 showed why: an ion drags its way through a liquid at a terminal speed set by its mobility). So a cell is built with large-area electrodes separated by a thin electrolyte, and metallic current collectors carry the electrons between the electrodes and the terminals. A cylindrical cell is exactly that stack, wound into a roll; coin, prismatic and flat cells hold the same layers in other shapes."],[2,"f","f2-3","Figure 2.3","An electrode is a coating, not a block. Try this: follow our ion from the top down through the pores, and the electrons up the carbon from the foil, and find the particle where they meet."],[2,"p","f2-3","figure 2.3","An electrode is not a solid block of the active material. It is a coating: particles of active material, a conductive diluent such as carbon powder, and a polymer binder that holds the mixture together and bonds it to the current collector. Typically about 30 % of its volume is pores, and the pores are filled with electrolyte."],[2,"p","f2-3","figure 2.3","The porous structure extends the surface area where the reaction can happen and lowers the current density, so the cell works more efficiently. A particle can only react where electrons reach it through the carbon and ions reach it through the electrolyte; a later module returns to what happens where one of them does not."],[2,"f","f6-1","Figure 2.4","The rocking chair: graphite against LiCoO2, today’s form of the carbon | LiCoO2 cell that Sony put on the market in 1991 (Sony’s first cell used a coke-type carbon). Try this: let it run until the graphite is empty, then press Charge and follow our ion back home."],[2,"p","f6-1","figure 2.4","Let us open your cell and look at the two electrodes. The first thing to notice is that neither of them is used up. Both are insertion hosts: solids that conduct ions and electrons and let guest ions in and out by a solid-state redox reaction. Because their structure and shape survive, insertion electrodes are highly reversible; for layered hosts the process is called intercalation."],[2,"p","f6-1","figure 2.4","This is what sets the cell apart from its predecessors. Replacing their lithium-metal negative electrode with a second insertion host gave the lithium-ion or rocking-chair cell: demonstrated by Murphy and by Scrosati, put on the market by Sony in June 1991 as carbon against LiCoO2. In the graphite, lithium sits between the carbon sheets, up to one lithium per six carbons, LiC6."],[2,"p","f6-1","figure 2.4","In LiCoO2 it sits between the oxide layers, and here the host sets a limit: LiCoO2 still gives up only about half its lithium in practice, about 135 to 140 mAh/g, because its structure degrades at high states of charge: once more than about 0.55 of the lithium is removed, the oxide starts to give off oxygen. Consumer cells have been pushing the upper cut-off voltage higher to win more capacity."],[2,"p","f6-1","figure 2.4","Nothing is consumed in an ideal lithium-ion cell: the ions only change address."],[2,"f","f6-5","Figure 2.5","From a chemist’s curiosity to a product, and on to today’s cell. Try this: step through the nine events and find the one that removed lithium metal from the cell, and the one that made graphite usable."],[2,"p","f6-5","figure 2.5","We now know what is inside the cell: two hosts, a rocking guest and a protective layer. How did it come together? The pieces arrived one at a time. In 1972, at a conference at Belgirate, Steele singled out layered dichalcogenides such as TiS2 as positive electrodes and Armand set out what an intercalation electrode needs; in 1976 lithium went reversibly into TiS2 against a lithium-metal electrode. Exxon’s cell then failed on dendrites that shorted it and ignited the electrolyte. Armand’s answer in the late 1970s was the rocking chair, and Lazzari and Scrosati built the first one, LiₓWO2 against TiS2, at about 2 V."],[2,"p","f6-5","figure 2.5","Around 1980 Goodenough’s group showed that over half the lithium could be taken out of LiCoO2 reversibly, at about 4 V, so that a cell could be built discharged. In 1983 Yoshino found that petroleum coke cycled in a carbonate electrolyte and proposed carbon against LiCoO2; Sony’s cell of June 1991 delivered about 80 Wh/kg and 200 Wh/L, more than any other rechargeable of the time."],[2,"p","f6-5","figure 2.5","With the LiPF6 in EC/DMC electrolyte proposed in 1993 and decades of refinement, the same chemistry reached about 250 Wh/kg and 600 Wh/L by 2020. Today, cells with NMC-type (lithium nickel manganese cobalt oxide) positive electrodes typically reach 300 Wh/kg and above 700 Wh/L, while state-of-the-art LiFePO4 cells rose from 90 Wh/kg in 2010 to 160 Wh/kg in 2022. Since its launch the lithium-ion cell has gained a factor of three to four in energy density while its price fell by a factor of 18."],[2,"p","f6-5","figure 2.5","You can now explain the parts of a cell and their jobs, why the separator must never let the electrodes touch, why cells are built from thin, wide layers, and why an electrode is a porous coating rather than a block."],[2,"p","f6-5","figure 2.5","Two of those parts decide how hard the push is."],[2,"p","f6-5","figure 2.5","Murray, Hall and Dahn built full coin cells with graphite and a nickel-rich oxide and found that three “identical” cells kept 95 %, 90 % and 80 % of their capacity after 100 cycles. Taking them apart showed the cause: the positive and negative discs were slightly misaligned, and lithium had plated on the parts of the positive electrode not covered by the negative one. The spring and spacer had been dropped in at an angle from tweezers; placing them from directly above with a vacuum pen, and using a thicker, more compliant separator, brought the cells to the reproducibility of machine-made pouch cells. Why plated lithium is lost for good is a story for a later module."],[3,"p","","","Why does a lithium-ion cell give over 3.6 V and a torch cell 1.5 V?"],[3,"p","","","Where we are. Chapter 1 measured 1.10 V between zinc and copper and showed that it sits in two jumps, one at each metal|liquid boundary. Chapter 2 named the parts. Now the physics of a jump: what a potential means for the electrons in a metal, what happens at the boundary, and why the two electrodes of your cell sit where they do on a ladder."],[3,"f","f2-4","Figure 3.1","Different chemistries, different voltages. Try this: hover or tap a bar to see the two electrodes and the electrolyte inside; then find the highest bar with a green dot."],[3,"p","f2-4","figure 3.1","The same four parts, in different materials, give very different cells. The table behind this chart lists the systems on the market in 2004: zinc and manganese dioxide in the 1.5 V cells of a torch, lead and lead oxide in the 2.0 V cells of a car battery, and graphite with lithium cobalt oxide in the 4.0 V cells of a phone."],[3,"p","f2-4","figure 3.1","Two patterns are already visible: every system with a water-based electrolyte stays at or below 2 V, and every system above 2 V is built on lithium with a nonaqueous electrolyte. Where the voltage comes from, and why water sets a ceiling, is the next question."],[3,"f","f3-1","Figure 3.2","Potential as electron energy. Moving an electrode’s potential moves the energy of its electrons, one electron-volt per volt, the other way. Try this: slide slowly negative until the first electron jumps, note the potential, then slide positive until one jumps back."],[3,"p","f3-1","figure 3.2","In chapter 0 a potential was energy per unit charge. For an electrode that has a sharp meaning: to observe or control the potential of an electrode is to observe or control the energy of its transferable electrons. Drive an electrode to a more negative potential and the energy of its electrons is raised; for each volt, each electron gains one electron-volt, 96.5 kJ per mole of electrons, comparable to the energies of chemical bonds."],[3,"p","f3-1","figure 3.2","The energy of those transferable electrons has a name: the Fermi energy, or Fermi level. In a metal the electron states form a continuum, filled from the bottom up; the Fermi level marks the division between filled and empty, and because empty states lie just above it, an electron can move with almost no activation, which is what makes a metal conduct."],[3,"p","f3-1","figure 3.2","Raise the Fermi level above an empty orbital of a species in the electrolyte and electrons flow from the electrode into the solution, a reduction current; lower it below a filled orbital and electrons flow from the solution onto the electrode, an oxidation current."],[3,"p","f3-1","figure 3.2","This is the same fact as the potential ladder further down: a more negative electrode holds its electrons at a higher energy, so it is the stronger reducing agent. Lithium, at the bottom of every ladder, holds them highest of all."],[3,"p","f3-1","figure 3.2","Electrode potential is electron energy in disguise: more negative means higher-energy electrons, one electron-volt per volt."],[3,"f","f3-2","Figure 3.3","Inside one jump: the electrical double layer. Try this: press “Replay the formation” and watch the charge separate and the jump grow with it; then slide the charge to positive and watch the ions in the liquid swap sign."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","Now zoom into one of the jumps of figure 1.1. Put a metal into an electrolyte and the two phases exchange a little charge until their electron energies match; equality of the Fermi energies of metal and solution is the condition for equilibrium, and electrons flow from the phase with the higher Fermi energy to the one with the lower until it is reached. The amount of charge that moves is tiny, so the composition of the solution does not noticeably change."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","Here is how that field is built, step by step. Dip a piece of zinc into a solution of its own ions. A zinc ion sitting in the metal and a zinc ion dissolved in the liquid are not in the same state: their electrochemical potentials differ, and a species moves across a boundary in the direction that lowers its electrochemical potential until it is the same on both sides."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","So a few Zn2+ ions leave the metal and enter the liquid, and each leaves its two electrons behind. Now the metal is slightly negative and the liquid touching it slightly positive. Those separated charges are the field: the excess electrons sit on the metal’s surface, the excess cations gather in the liquid right against it, and between two sheets of opposite charge a molecular distance apart runs a strong field, pointing from the liquid into the metal. Every ion that crosses makes that field a little stronger, and the field pulls back on the next ion that tries; the crossing stops when the electrical pull exactly balances the chemical push, which is the equilibrium of equal electrochemical potentials."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","At a copper electrode in copper sulfate the exchange runs the other way, ions from the liquid plating onto the metal and leaving it positive. Where the exchange is of electrons rather than ions, as at a platinum wire in a solution of iron ions, the rule is the same: electrons cross from the phase with the higher Fermi energy to the one with the lower until the two are equal."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","In every case the amount of charge that crosses is tiny, far too small to change the composition of the liquid, yet it sets up a potential difference of a volt or so across a few nanometres."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","That charge cannot spread through the metal: a conductor at rest keeps its excess charge on its surface (chapter 0), and on an electrode it lies in a layer thinner than a nanometre. The liquid answers with an equal and opposite charge right at the boundary, an excess of cations or of anions."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","The whole array, charged metal surface, oriented solvent molecules, a compact layer of ions one solvent shell out and a diffuse layer beyond, is the electrical double layer. Above 0.01 M the diffuse part is less than about 10 nm thick."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","Why does the field stay inside that thin layer and not reach out into the liquid? Inside the metal it is zero for the reason chapter 0 gave: any field in a conductor at rest would move the free charges until it was gone. On the liquid side the Coulomb field of the metal’s surface charge is almost entirely cancelled by the polarization of the electrolyte: ions of the opposite sign crowd in and solvent molecules turn, and a few nanometres out the field has been screened to nothing, so the bulk of the liquid sits at one potential."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","That is why the strip under figure 1.1 is flat through the solution at open circuit. The liquid can even hold a potential step with no excess charge at all: water molecules touching a metal tend to line up, and a layer of aligned dipoles is itself a charge separation."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","Draw current and one thing changes. A gentle field appears through the bulk liquid, the slope of the potential that drives the ions, and it is small because the electrolyte conducts well; this is the ohmic drop. The two interfacial fields remain, shifted a little from their rest values because each reaction needs an extra push, an overpotential, to run at a finite rate."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","So the field in a working cell has three parts: a strong, nanometre-thin one at each electrode, built by the chemistry itself out of a minute charge separation and held in place by the electrolyte’s screening, and a weak one across the liquid that exists only while current flows."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","Two sheets of opposite charge a molecular distance apart: the potential steps across them, and the field inside the step can reach 107 V/cm, strong enough to distort the molecules that sit in it. The arrangement behaves like a capacitor, q = C·E, with 10 to 40 µF for every square centimetre of electrode, and it forms and relaxes in about 10−8 s. A cell’s voltage is the sum of such steps, taken along the path from one electrode to the other."],[3,"p","f3-2","figure 3.3","A cell’s voltage lives in two double layers, each a few nanometres thick, built by a tiny charge separation that the chemistry itself creates."],[3,"f","f3-3","Figure 3.4","The potential ladder. Pick a negative and a positive electrode and read the gap. Your choice is remembered and carried into the next modules. Try this: guess which pair gives the most voltage before you choose; then switch on the water window and see how many rungs a water-based cell can never reach."],[3,"p","f3-3","figure 3.4","Chemists carry the same idea one step further. The energy of a charged species in a phase has two parts: a chemical part, from its surroundings on the scale of a molecule, and an electrical part, its charge times the local potential. Their sum is the electrochemical potential, μ̄ = μ + zFφ, and a species is at equilibrium between two phases when its electrochemical potential is the same in both."],[3,"p","f3-3","figure 3.4","Apply that to the electrons in the two electrodes of a cell and the open-circuit voltage falls out: it is the difference between the electrochemical potentials of the electrons in the two electrodes, divided by the electron charge, VOC = (μA − μC)/e."],[3,"p","f3-3","figure 3.4","So picture each material as a rung on a ladder of electron energy, drawn as a potential: the cell voltage is the gap between the two rungs you choose. Battery scientists measure the rungs against lithium metal, which is why every value here reads “versus Li/Li+”. Lithium sits at the bottom. In Tarascon and Armand’s words it is “the most electropositive” metal, the one with the most negative standard potential, −3.04 V against the standard hydrogen electrode, and it is also the lightest metal, at 6.94 g/mol and 0.53 g/cm3."],[3,"p","f3-3","figure 3.4","The gap has a ceiling, and the ceiling is the electrolyte. Water is thermodynamically stable over only 1.23 V, between the potential at which it gives off hydrogen and the potential at which it gives off oxygen; slow kinetics stretch that to about 2 V in practice, which is why cells with an aqueous electrolyte and a long shelf life stop near 1.5 V."],[3,"p","f3-3","figure 3.4","But where that 1.23 V sits matters as much as its width: at pH 0 it runs from 3.05 to 4.27 V versus lithium, and it slides 59 mV lower for every pH unit. Every rung below about 3 V, which is every negative electrode on the ladder and most of the positive ones, is simply out of reach for water. The organic carbonate electrolytes of lithium-ion cells are stable from about 1.1 V to about 4.3 V versus lithium, about 4.6 V in practice, and there is the answer to the hook: not a stronger reaction, but an electrolyte that tolerates a wider gap, in the right place."],[3,"p","f3-3","figure 3.4","Two reservoirs at different heights: the pressure at the pipe between them depends on the height difference, not on how wide the reservoirs are. The picture breaks as soon as water flows: the height stays, but the flow costs pressure. The next module to come is about that cost."],[3,"p","f3-3","figure 3.4","Voltage is a property of the pair of materials, and the electrolyte sets how far apart they may be."],[3,"f","f3-4","Figure 3.5","The same gap as a picture of electron energy. The figure follows your cell from figure 3.3. Try this: choose lithium titanate in figure 3.4 and watch the warning on the left vanish; then choose the 4.75 V spinel and watch one appear on the right."],[3,"p","f3-4","figure 3.5","The ceiling has a mechanism. An electrolyte is a collection of molecules, and each has a highest filled and a lowest empty energy level; the gap between them is the electrolyte window. A negative electrode whose electrons sit above the empty level will reduce the electrolyte, and a positive electrode whose empty states sit below the filled level will oxidize it, unless a passivating layer forms at the surface and blocks the reaction."],[3,"p","f3-4","figure 3.5","Graphite sits about 0.2 V above lithium, outside the carbonate window, so a layer must form on it during the first charge; lithium titanate at 1.5 V sits inside the window and needs none. A later module follows that layer as it forms."],[3,"p","f3-4","figure 3.5","Notice the path of the electron in the figure. It gives up its e·VOC only by travelling round the external circuit, from the negative electrode to the positive one; it never crosses the electrolyte, which has no free electrons (chapter 1). Inside the cell it is our ion that crosses, keeping every region neutral."],[3,"f","f3-5","Figure 3.6","Why oxide hosts give 4 V and sulfide hosts about 2.5 V. The redox couple of the transition metal can be lowered only as far as the top of the anion band. Try this: step through the four callouts and check that the oxide’s 4 V sits just above the electrolyte’s 4.3 eV line."],[3,"p","f3-5","figure 3.6","Why do oxide positive electrodes reach 4 V when the sulfide of the first rechargeable lithium cell reached only 2.2 V? In a solid the electron levels of the many atoms merge into bands, continuous ranges of energy, filled from the bottom up. The redox couple of the host’s transition metal cannot be pushed below the top of the host’s filled anion band. In the layered sulfides that top sits about 2.5 eV below the lithium level; in the layered oxides it sits about 4.0 eV below, which is why oxide hosts are used in today’s lithium-ion cells. Polyanions such as phosphate pull the band lower still through what chemists call the inductive effect, which is how LiFePO4 reaches 3.5 V."],[3,"f","f3-6","Figure 3.7","The same gap in the language of thermodynamics. Try this: double the voltage and double the electrons, and watch which one the energy bar follows; then set n = 2, E = 1.10 V and find the Daniell mark."],[3,"p","f3-6","figure 3.7","In the language of thermodynamics the same gap reads ΔG = −nFE: the free energy of a reaction, the energy it can deliver as work, equals the charge it moves per mole, nF, times the cell voltage, with the minus sign saying that a spontaneous reaction has a positive voltage."],[3,"p","f3-6","figure 3.7","So the voltage is the reaction’s free energy per unit of charge, and nF, the total charge available, is its capacity factor. The Faraday constant is the charge carried by one mole of electrons, 96 485.3 coulombs."],[3,"p","f3-6","figure 3.7","You can now explain why a more negative electrode is the stronger reducing agent, where a cell’s voltage physically sits, why lithium cells have the highest voltages, why water caps a cell near 1.5 V and where on the lithium scale that cap lies, why oxide hosts beat sulfides, and what ΔG = −nFE means."],[3,"p","f3-6","figure 3.7","You have chosen a voltage. How much charge can your cell hold?"],[3,"p","f3-6","figure 3.7","For electrons in a metal the electrochemical potential is μ̄e = μ°e − Fφ, with no activity term because the electron concentration never changes appreciably. Touch a copper wire to a zinc electrode and electrons move between them until their electrochemical potentials are equal, which leaves a potential difference φZn − φCu = (μ°eZn − μ°eCu)/F between the two metals: a contact potential, one of the steps in figure 1.1’s profile. Write the equilibrium of the whole cell reaction in electrochemical potentials and the terms in φ cancel everywhere except at the two copper leads, giving −nFE = n(μ̄eCu′ − μ̄eCu) and, on expanding the chemical potentials, the Nernst equation. Per electron, the same statement is Goodenough and Park’s VOC = (μA − μC)/e."],[3,"p","f3-6","figure 3.7","A potential is always a difference, so every value needs a reference. Textbook tables use the standard hydrogen electrode, against which lithium sits at −3.045 V; battery papers use lithium metal itself as the zero, which is why the same LiCoO2 electrode reads about 4.0 V “versus Li/Li+” and about 1 V versus hydrogen. In a lithium-ion cell the negative electrode is not lithium metal but a host that holds lithium near lithium’s potential, so the cell voltage is a little less than the positive electrode’s ladder value: graphite costs about 0.2 V."],[4,"p","","","What do mAh, Wh and Wh/kg mean, and how long would your cell run a lamp?"],[4,"p","","","Where we are. Chapter 3 gave your cell a voltage: the gap between two rungs, which is the free energy of its reaction per unit of charge. Now the other factor: how much charge the materials hold. Charge times voltage is energy; energy per second is power."],[4,"f","f4-1","Figure 4.1","The Faraday calculator. Pick a material, or type your own molar mass and electron count. Try this: pick lithium metal and see why it tempts every battery designer; then pick your positive electrode and multiply by your cell’s voltage."],[4,"p","f4-1","figure 4.1","Capacity is a count of electrons. Faraday’s law ties the amount of material transformed to the charge that flows, Q = nFN for N moles of reactant, and it has “no known exceptions”. Turn it around: a gram of a material of molar mass M that gives up n electrons per formula unit holds nF/M coulombs, which the battery world writes in milliampere-hours per gram, Q = nF/(3.6 M). The Faraday constant is the charge of one mole of electrons, 96 485.3 C or 26.8015 Ah."],[4,"p","f4-1","figure 4.1","Two conventions hide in that formula. Graphite’s 372 mAh/g refers to the mass of carbon, the host as assembled, not to the mass of LiC6: Tarascon and Armand give 372 mAh/g for the end compound LiC6 and about 350 mAh/g in practice. Positive-electrode materials are referred to their lithiated mass: LiFePO4 holds 170 mAh/g in theory and is used at about 90 % of it, while LiCoO2 is limited to about half of its lithium for safety, 140 mAh/g in practice."],[4,"p","f4-1","figure 4.1","Light elements and many electrons per formula unit give high capacity; high voltage turns capacity into energy."],[4,"f","f4-2","Figure 4.2","Energy is the area under the discharge curve. The curve follows your cell’s positive electrode: flat for LiFePO4, sloping for the others. Try this: slide the rate up to 5C and watch the area shrink from both sides, lower and shorter."],[4,"p","f4-2","figure 4.2","Energy is the integral of the voltage over the charge delivered: on a discharge curve, the area under the line, which is also the average voltage times the capacity. Per kilogram it is called specific energy (Wh/kg), per litre energy density (Wh/L); how fast it can be delivered is specific power (W/kg) and power density (W/L)."],[4,"p","f4-2","figure 4.2","The capacity itself depends on how hard you pull. At high current the transfer of ions across the interfaces becomes diffusion-limited and the cell reaches less of its charge before the cut-off; that part of the loss is reversible and comes back at a gentler rate. The next module to come takes the curve apart."],[4,"f","f4-3","Figure 4.3","The Ragone map: energy against power. Regions only; the axes carry no numbers because the source figure is itself simplified. Try this: find where a thin-film battery would sit, then read why the engine is off on its own."],[4,"p","f4-3","figure 4.3","To compare the two abilities, energy and power, engineers use the Ragone plot. On it, supercapacitors are high-power systems, fuel cells high-energy systems, and batteries sit between them, overlapping both: a battery with thin-film electrodes reaches the power of a supercapacitor. No single electrochemical system matches the combustion engine on both axes at once, which is why the review suggests combining them: batteries or supercapacitors for the power, fuel cells for the energy."],[4,"p","f4-3","figure 4.3","One caution before you trust any Wh/kg figure: the theoretical energy of a chemistry, reckoned on the mass of everything that reacts, is never what a cell delivers. Practical values are significantly lower, and Winter and Brodd list among the causes the inert parts a cell needs (conductive diluents, current collectors, containers), the internal resistances of electrodes, electrolyte and contacts, and incomplete use of the active masses."],[4,"p","f4-3","figure 4.3","Chapter 2 showed the inert parts; chapter 0 showed why resistance costs voltage, and therefore energy, whenever current flows. How large the gap is depends on the chemistry and on how the cell is built, so this page quotes delivered values only where a source gives them."],[4,"p","f4-3","figure 4.3","To give the numbers a feel, Winter and Brodd note that a specific energy of 30 Wh/kg means one kilogram of lead-acid battery can power a 60 W lamp for only half an hour. The first commercial lithium-ion cell of 1991 gave over 3.6 V and 120 to 150 Wh/kg, two to three times the usual nickel-cadmium cell of the day."],[4,"p","f4-3","figure 4.3","You can now explain where a capacity in mAh/g comes from and which mass it refers to, why energy is an area, what the Ragone plot shows, and why a cell delivers less than its chemistry promises."],[4,"p","f4-3","figure 4.3","Run Faraday’s law on the lithiated compound LiC6 (79.0 g/mol) and you get 339 mAh/g; run it on the carbon host C6 (72.07 g/mol) and you get 372 mAh/g. Both are correct; they answer different questions. The convention in the field is to refer negative-electrode hosts to their delithiated mass and positive-electrode materials to their lithiated mass, which is how each is weighed when the cell is built. Whenever you compare capacities, check the basis first."],[5,"p","","","What happens when we charge a cell and use it, and why does it get warm?"],[5,"p","","","Where we are. In chapter 4 we counted the charge your cell can hold and turned it into energy, with the cell mostly at rest. Now we let the current flow and ask what that costs. We start at the test bench, with how a charge and a discharge are actually done and recorded. Then we follow what current costs (voltage, capacity and heat), why some curves are flat and others slope, what happens inside real electrodes made of many particles, how a curve can be read for signs of ageing, and how many cycles a cell survives. From here on we follow one lithium ion, our ion, through the cell."],[5,"f","f5-1","Figure 5.1","One full test of your cell, as a cycler runs it. Try this: slide the current from C/10 to 5C and watch the time axis shrink, the discharge stop earlier and the constant-voltage step grow longer."],[5,"p","f5-1","figure 5.1","Let us connect your cell to something that uses it. A discharge is the cell doing work: current flows out through a lamp, a phone or a motor. A charge is the reverse: a charger pushes current back in, in the opposite direction, and restores the cell to its charged condition. On discharge the negative electrode is oxidized and the positive electrode is reduced; charging runs both reactions backwards. In a lithium-ion cell that means lithium ions leave one electrode, cross the electrolyte and enter the other, while their electrons go round through the wire (chapter 2)."],[5,"s","s-the-test-bench","The test bench",""],[5,"p","s-the-test-bench","The test bench","To watch this happen we put the cell on a test bench. Cells are studied, and their capacities measured, on a cycler: an instrument that sets the current and records the voltage at regular intervals (once a second in one set of tests). Most tests hold the current constant. This is galvanostatic cycling, and its record, the voltage against the charge passed, is the raw material of everything else in this module."],[5,"p","s-the-test-bench","The test bench","At constant current the charge passed is simply current times time, Q=It . Currents are quoted as C-rates: 1C is the current that passes the cell’s full nominal charge in one hour, so C/20 takes twenty hours. In one study, for example, a 210 mAh cell was recorded at C/20, that is at 10.5 mA."],[5,"p","s-the-test-bench","The test bench","A step cannot run for ever. Each step stops at a cut-off voltage. Overcharging or overdischarging drives unwanted reactions that cost capacity. Charging often ends with a constant-voltage step: the cycler holds the upper cut-off, the current falls away by itself, and the step ends when it reaches a small limit. In two published protocols the cycler held 4.2 V until the current had fallen to C/20 in one and to C/25 in the other."],[5,"p","s-the-test-bench","The test bench","A test is a recipe of currents, limits and rests; the cell answers with its voltage."],[5,"f","f5-2","Figure 5.2","Where the voltage goes when current flows. The open-circuit voltage is your cell’s, from the ladder of chapter 3. Try this: switch between Discharge and Charge and watch the two jumps on the strip shrink, then grow; then slide the current up and watch the slope steepen."],[5,"p","f5-2","figure 5.2","The cycler shows us only the voltage at the terminals. Let us look along the cell to see where that voltage goes. At rest, a cell’s voltage is the sum of the potential jumps at its two electrode-electrolyte boundaries (chapter 3). Draw a current and two things change. The ions must be driven through the electrolyte, which has a resistance, so the potential now slopes across it, cations running down the slope and anions up it: the ohmic drop, IR . And each boundary has to be pushed off its equilibrium before its reaction keeps pace with the current; that extra push is the electrode’s overpotential."],[5,"p","f5-2","figure 5.2","For the whole cell this comes down to two lines. On discharge the terminals give the open-circuit voltage minus a polarization η ; on charge the charger must supply the open-circuit voltage plus it:"],[5,"p","f5-2","figure 5.2","The polarization itself has three parts: activation, from the charge-transfer reactions at the interfaces; ohmic, from the resistance of the cell’s components and contacts; and concentration, from the limits of mass transport."],[5,"p","f5-2","figure 5.2","Polarization or overpotential? You will meet both. Here polarization belongs to the whole cell, η=EOCV−ET , the open-circuit voltage minus the voltage under load; overpotential, η=E−Eeq , belongs to one electrode and has its own sign. We keep them apart."],[5,"p","f5-2","figure 5.2","Current costs voltage: a working cell gives less than its open-circuit voltage, and charging it takes more."],[5,"f","f5-3","Figure 5.3","The voltage curve, computed, with the polarization drawn on the plot. The shape follows your cell: flat for LiFePO4, sloping for the oxides. Try this: put the probe at 90 % and slide the current from C/10 to 5C; watch which part in the right-hand panel grows fastest and where the discharge now stops."],[5,"p","f5-3","figure 5.3","Now let us record the voltage while the current runs, all the way from full to empty: that record is the discharge curve. Its typical shape carries all three polarizations, and it tells us the capacity, the effect of rate and temperature, and the health of the cell. The upper end of the curve has its own story. A LiCoO2 electrode, for instance, gives up only about half its lithium. In practice that is about 135 to 140 mAh/g, because its structure degrades at high states of charge. Consumer cells have been pushing the upper cut-off voltage higher to win more capacity."],[5,"p","f5-3","figure 5.3","Push harder and the curve pays twice. The polarization grows with the current, so the two branches move apart; and the discharge reaches its cut-off sooner, before all the charge is out. That missing capacity is not lost for good: at high current the transfer of ions becomes diffusion-limited, and what the cell could not deliver comes back at a gentler rate."],[5,"p","f5-3","figure 5.3","The gap between the charge and discharge branches is energy paid as heat on every cycle, and it widens with current."],[5,"f","f5-4","Figure 5.4","Switch the current off and the three polarizations leave on three different clocks. Try this: press Play and watch the time cursor; the curve stays put for a long while after the first jump and then moves twice more."],[5,"p","f5-4","figure 5.4","If the three parts all add to one gap, how can we tell them apart? By their clocks. Ohmic polarization appears and disappears almost instantly, within about a microsecond. Activation polarization builds up and decays in about 10−4 to 10−2 s. Concentration polarization is slow: 10−2 s or longer. So let us switch the current off and time the recovery: the step is ohmic, the fast relaxation activation, the slow drift concentration. Interrupting the current in this way is how the polarizations are separated in practice."],[5,"p","f5-4","figure 5.4","The middle clock is set by the charge-transfer reaction itself. A reaction that runs fast in both directions at equilibrium, one with a large exchange current, needs only a small overpotential to carry a given current, and battery reactions with exchange current densities around 10−2 A/cm2 are favoured. We meet the same clocks again in Lab 3, read with an alternating current."],[5,"p","f5-4","figure 5.4","Ohmic, activation, concentration: instant, milliseconds, tens of milliseconds and longer."],[5,"f","f5-11","Figure 5.5","Where the heat comes from, through one cycle of your cell. Try this: flip the sign of dE/dT and watch only the green line change; then compare C/10 with 5C."],[5,"p","f5-11","figure 5.5","One cost of current is still to be followed: the energy lost on every cycle. The lost energy becomes heat, of two kinds. An irreversible part, often loosely called Joule heat, comes from the whole polarization: it is released whichever way the current flows. A reversible part comes from the entropy change of the reaction, and its sign follows the temperature coefficient of the voltage, dE/dT . If it is positive the cell heats on charge and cools on discharge, as nickel-cadmium does; lead-acid has a negative coefficient and does the opposite. Bernardi’s energy balance writes the two side by side:"],[5,"p","f5-11","figure 5.5","The heat is released inside the cell, at the electrode surfaces, so a cell meant for high currents has to be built to shed it. Because the entropic term changes sign with the current, the heat it takes in on one half of a cycle it gives back on the other; the irreversible term only ever adds up."],[5,"p","f5-11","figure 5.5","You can now explain how a cycler charges and discharges a cell and what a C-rate is, why a working cell gives less than its open-circuit voltage and why charging takes more, what the three polarizations are and how fast each relaxes, how a battery’s curve differs from a capacitor’s, why some curves are flat and others slope, why a flat curve tilts at high rate and can remember its past, why fast discharges leave material unused, how slopes of the curve reveal ageing, why 99.9 % per cycle is not enough, and where the lost energy goes."],[5,"p","f5-11","figure 5.5","So far we have watched the cell from outside, through its voltage. To see why the curve has the shape it has, let us follow our ion inside the cell."],[5,"p","f5-11","figure 5.5","For one electrode, the current and the overpotential η=E−Eeq are linked by the Butler-Volmer equation. Written with reduction current positive, it reads"],[5,"p","f5-11","figure 5.5","with α the transfer coefficient (between 0.3 and 0.7 in most cases) and i0 the exchange current. It holds while the current stays below about 10 % of the limiting current, so that mass transport does not interfere. Near equilibrium the exponentials can be expanded and the current is simply proportional to η : the electrode behaves like a resistor, the charge-transfer resistance Rct=RT/(Fi0) for a one-electron reaction. A large exchange current means a small Rct ."],[5,"p","f5-11","figure 5.5","Far from equilibrium one exponential wins and η grows with the logarithm of the current, the Tafel relation η=a+blogi , which holds once |η| exceeds about 118 mV at 25 °C; the same empirical form is quoted for batteries. In the battery literature it is written with oxidation current positive (the printed equation lost a bracket and a sign); the physics is the same with the roles of α and 1−α swapped."],[5,"p","f5-11","figure 5.5","One caution about the heat equation. It takes the open-circuit voltage U from a slow measurement and dU/dT from small temperature steps, and in one set of measurements it estimated heat reasonably during continuous discharge but overestimated it during pulsed discharge, by up to 26 % for a LiFePO4 cell and 49 % for an NMC cell."],[6,"p","","","Why is the curve of LiFePO4 flat while that of LiCoO2 slopes?"],[6,"f","f5-5","Figure 6.1","Two ways to store charge, and the curves they draw. Try this: in the real supercapacitor, halve the current and check that the whole picture shrinks by half, the ramp included."],[6,"p","f5-5","figure 6.1","We have seen how current pulls the curve away from the open-circuit voltage. But why does a battery’s curve have the shape it has in the first place? Let us start from two extremes. An ideal battery material is one that supplies all its capacity through one uniform chemical process at one fixed potential. Its charge per volt, the differential capacity dq/dU , is a single spike, and the energy it stores is simply proportional to the charge."],[6,"p","f5-5","figure 6.1","The opposite extreme is an ideal capacitor material: its differential capacity is constant, its specific capacitance Csp , so the potential rises in a straight line as charge goes in."],[6,"p","f5-5","figure 6.1","“Ideal” here means a mathematical limit, not the best material for a job. And “capacitor material” covers more than capacitors: double layers, surface reactions, and insertion electrodes that take lithium as a single phase all behave this way to some degree. For any real material, a capacity only means something together with the voltage window it was measured in."],[6,"s","s-a-real-supercapacitor","A real supercapacitor",""],[6,"p","s-a-real-supercapacitor","A real supercapacitor","Real capacitors are not ideal either; let us see how one departs from the straight line. In one study, three commercial double-layer capacitors were charged and discharged at constant current and their curves read with a small circuit. It has a resistance RH in series with the capacitance C , plus an inner branch, a resistance Ri in parallel with a capacitance Ci , attributed to the diffusion of ions in the capacitor. For a current I0 switched on at t=0 :"],[6,"p","s-a-real-supercapacitor","A real supercapacitor","So the voltage jumps by RHI0 the moment the current starts, then bends for a fraction of a second before settling to a straight line of slope I0/C . After about five time constants it runs along that straight asymptote, which starts from (RH+Ri)I0 ."],[6,"p","s-a-real-supercapacitor","A real supercapacitor","Reverse the current and the voltage drops at once by 2RHI0 , because the current has changed by twice I0 . The bend that follows is twice as large as at the start, and the measurements showed that 2 : 1 ratio on all three capacitors (for the 1 F one at 10 mA, 0.34 mV on charge and 0.69 mV on discharge; the fitted circuit gives larger bends, so only the ratio is compared here). The inner capacitance came out close to two sevenths of the main one."],[6,"p","s-a-real-supercapacitor","A real supercapacitor","A battery stores charge at a fixed potential, a capacitor in proportion to its voltage; real electrodes sit between the two."],[6,"f","f5-6","Figure 6.2","Plateau or slope: the Gibbs phase rule on a measured curve. Try this: press Step until the dot is on the plateau, then count the dark blue particles; keep stepping and watch the count rise while the voltage stays put."],[6,"p","f5-6","figure 6.2","Real battery electrodes sit between the two extremes, and their curves differ: some are flat, others slope. Why? The answer is the Gibbs phase rule, which counts the freedoms F left to a system of C components in P phases:"],[6,"p","f5-6","figure 6.2","When the reaction turns one solid phase into another, two phases coexist. With temperature and pressure fixed nothing is left free, so the voltage stays constant: a plateau. When lithium dissolves into a single phase, the composition stays free and the voltage slopes with it."],[6,"p","f5-6","figure 6.2","A simple example makes the difference concrete. An electrode with the overall composition Li0.75M can be one uniform phase of that composition, or half Li0.5M and half LiM. In the second case, adding lithium only changes the proportions of two fixed phases; materials that split like this are often called staging materials."],[6,"s","s-lifepo-measured","LiFePO4, measured",""],[6,"p","s-lifepo-measured","LiFePO4, measured","LiFePO4 is the classic two-phase case. Most of its range is a mixture of a lithium-poor and a lithium-rich phase. Its open-circuit curve, measured as the average of a charge and a discharge at C/100 and fitted, is flat at 3.43 V vs Li over the middle of the range and slopes at both ends, where the material takes lithium as a partial solid solution."],[6,"p","s-lifepo-measured","LiFePO4, measured","Even this plateau is not perfectly single-valued. A gap of about 20 mV remains between the charge and discharge voltages even at C/1000. That is a true, thermodynamic hysteresis, traced to the many particles of the electrode."],[6,"p","s-lifepo-measured","LiFePO4, measured","Other materials draw other shapes. The spinel LiMn2O4 shows steps near 4 V and a drop to 3 V. Which shape is better depends on the use: electronics prefer a flat, steady voltage, while a sloping curve makes it easy to read how much charge is left."],[6,"p","s-lifepo-measured","LiFePO4, measured","Two phases pin the voltage to a plateau; one phase lets it slope."],[6,"f","f5-7","Figure 6.3","One electrode, many particles: why the plateau tilts with rate, and why the electrode remembers its past. Try this: run a 1C discharge and watch group 4; then press “Same 50 %, two histories” and compare where the two charges end."],[6,"p","f5-7","figure 6.3","That hysteresis is our first hint that the many particles matter, so let us now take them seriously. Real electrodes are not one particle but billions, and they do not all react together. LiFePO4 conducts electrons very poorly, about 10−9 S/cm on its own, and is made usable by coating its particles with carbon and making them small. Each particle then gets its electrons through its contact with the conductive carbon, and some contacts are better than others."],[6,"p","f5-7","figure 6.3","A simple model builds that in, a resistive reactant: four groups of identical particles, differing only in their contact resistance. Its parameters were fitted to measured cells, and it does not try to describe the two phases inside a particle at all. With one set of parameters it reproduced those cells’ charge and discharge curves from C/25 to 1C, the first charge simulation of LiFePO4 its authors knew of."],[6,"s","s-why-the-plateau-tilts","Why the plateau tilts",""],[6,"p","s-why-the-plateau-tilts","Why the plateau tilts","The measured curves tilt more steeply in the middle as the rate rises. Since the open-circuit curve there is flat, that means the cell’s resistance grows as the charge or discharge goes on. The model explains why: the particles with the lowest resistance react first, the worse-connected ones later, and the faster the current, the stronger the effect."],[6,"p","s-why-the-plateau-tilts","Why the plateau tilts","The idea was tested directly. In LiFePO4 coin cells, adding one or two extra steel discs squeezed the electrode harder. The curves sloped less and the cells delivered more, and the fitted contact resistance of the worst group fell by up to 44 %."],[6,"p","s-why-the-plateau-tilts","Why the plateau tilts","LiFePO4 also gives more on charge than on discharge at the same rate, by 8 % at 3C and 11.5 % at 5C in those cells. The model captures this with a solid diffusion coefficient that is lower when the particle is full of lithium."],[6,"s","s-an-electrode-with-a-memory","An electrode with a memory",""],[6,"p","s-an-electrode-with-a-memory","An electrode with a memory","The groups have one more surprise for us. Bring the electrode to half charge in two ways, by charging from empty or discharging from full, rest it for two hours, then charge it at 1C. The two charges differ: 3.96 % in delivered capacity in those cells. This is path dependence, and it fades at low rates."],[6,"p","s-an-electrode-with-a-memory","An electrode with a memory","It has two ingredients. The particle groups end up holding different amounts of lithium, and nothing evens them out. Lithium moves between particles only where the open-circuit curve slopes, because only there does a fuller particle sit at a different voltage from an emptier one. On the plateau it barely moves, even after two hours of rest. Near 5 % charge, on the sloping end, the same experiment gave about a fifth of the effect."],[6,"p","s-an-electrode-with-a-memory","An electrode with a memory","A flat curve hides a crowd of particles reacting in turn; how well each is wired decides the rate, and the past decides the present."],[7,"p","","","When a cell is charged, where is the lithium stored?"],[7,"f","f6-2","Figure 7.1","The two hosts, atom by atom: graphite fills by stages; LiCoO2 gives up half its lithium. Try this: press the stage buttons from Stage 4 to Stage 1 and watch which gaps fill."],[7,"s","s-graphite-sheets-and-lithium-by-stages","Graphite: sheets, and lithium by stages",""],[7,"p","s-graphite-sheets-and-lithium-by-stages","Graphite: sheets, and lithium by stages","So far we have described each host in a sentence. Let us now look at them atom by atom, beginning with graphite. Graphite is a stack of graphene sheets: carbon atoms joined in hexagonal rings, the sheets held to one another by weak van der Waals forces and π–π interactions, 0.335 nm apart; in natural graphite they are mostly stacked in the order A, B, A, B. The lithium goes into the gaps between the sheets, at most one lithium for every six carbons, LiC6. The edges of the sheets are the favourable sites for lithium to go in, and it diffuses faster there; the flat faces, the basal planes, are less reactive."],[7,"p","s-graphite-sheets-and-lithium-by-stages","Graphite: sheets, and lithium by stages","The lithium does not fill all the gaps at once. It fills some gaps and leaves the others empty, forming stages: the stage number is the number of graphene sheets between two lithium layers. As the cell charges, the graphite passes from stage 4 through stage 3 to stage 2, LiC12, and finally stage 1, LiC6, with lithium in every gap. Stages 2 and 3 are stable phases that affect the equilibrium potential and the diffusion of lithium. Each change of stage is favoured thermodynamically but held back kinetically, the last one, from LiC12 to LiC6, most of all. In the Rüdorff-Hofmann picture each stage is made of complete lithium layers, as the figure draws them; in the Daumas-Hérold picture the guest forms islands within the layers instead. Full lithiation swells the graphite by about 10 %, mostly by the sheets moving apart, not by the sheets stretching."],[7,"s","s-licoo-lithium-between-oxide-sheets","LiCoO2: lithium between oxide sheets",""],[7,"p","s-licoo-lithium-between-oxide-sheets","LiCoO2: lithium between oxide sheets","Now we cross to the other side. LiCoO2 is a layered compound too: cobalt surrounded by oxygen in CoO2 sheets, with the lithium in the layer between two of them. The idea came from sodium, which Hagenmuller and co-workers had put between such sheets in the 1970s; in 1979 and 1980 Goodenough’s group made the lithium compound, at twice the voltage of TiS2. As we saw, only about half of its lithium is taken out on charge."],[7,"p","s-licoo-lithium-between-oxide-sheets","LiCoO2: lithium between oxide sheets","In both hosts, then, the sheets are what makes the cell work. Lithium comes and goes; the framework of carbon or of cobalt oxide stays, so the electrodes keep their structure and shape and the reaction can run back and forth many times."],[7,"p","s-licoo-lithium-between-oxide-sheets","LiCoO2: lithium between oxide sheets","Two layered solids and one guest: charging moves lithium from between the oxide sheets to between the carbon sheets, and discharging moves it back."],[7,"f","f6-4","Figure 7.2","Three hosts, three kinds of path for the ion. Try this: follow each ion for a few seconds and note which directions it never takes."],[7,"p","f6-4","figure 7.2","Our ion is now inside a host, protected by the SEI where it needs one. How does it move once it is in? The host decides. In the layered oxides lithium travels within two-dimensional planes; the spinel LiMn2O4 offers a three-dimensional network; in olivine LiFePO4 the paths are one-dimensional channels."],[7,"p","f6-4","figure 7.2","Inside the solid the ion moves by diffusion, down its concentration gradient, as Fick’s first law describes; in a time t it typically gets about (2Dt)1/2 from where it started, where D is its diffusion coefficient. Turned around, emptying a particle of size L takes a time of order L2/2D: halve the particle and it empties four times faster. We return to this in chapter 11, when the particles shrink to nanometres."],[7,"f","f9-2","Figure 7.3","Three ways to store lithium. Try this: play each mode and compare the shaded first-cycle loss and the gap between the two curves."],[7,"p","f9-2","figure 7.3","The families on the map differ in what happens to the material when lithium enters it, and there are three ways for that to go. In intercalation, lithium slips between the layers of a host that keeps its structure, as in graphite, Li+ + e− + xC ⇌ LiCx, or in titanium oxides; that is why these materials last for many cycles. In alloying, lithium forms an alloy with an element such as silicon, germanium or tin, xLi+ + xe− + M ⇌ LixM. In conversion, a compound is taken apart into metal and a lithium compound and rebuilt on the way back. Alloys and conversion materials tend to break into smaller particles as they cycle."],[7,"p","f9-2","figure 7.3","All three pay a price on the very first charge: they lose a large part of it, mostly to the SEI, which takes lithium from the positive electrode and lowers the first-cycle Coulombic efficiency. Alloys and conversion materials also show a large gap between the voltage at which lithium goes in and the voltage at which it comes out, which lowers the energy a full cell can deliver."],[7,"p","f9-2","figure 7.3","You can now explain where the lithium sits in graphite and in LiCoO2, why the cell is called a rocking chair, why a new cell loses lithium on its first charge and what it gets in return, how the host shapes the ion’s path, and how the cell came together between 1972 and 1996."],[7,"p","f9-2","figure 7.3","On every trip between the two hosts, our ion had to cross something to get there."],[8,"p","","","What is the liquid inside, and why these molecules?"],[8,"p","","","Where we are. Chapter 7 put our ion in its two hosts and sent it from one to the other. Between them it crosses the electrolyte, the part of the cell that must carry ions and stop electrons. What is that medium, and how does an ion get through it? We start from nothing: what the liquid in a lithium-ion cell is made of, how its molecules sit around each ion, how an ion moves through it, what the electric field inside it looks like, and how the arrangement changes, layer by layer, as we approach an electrode. Then we compare the liquid with polymers and ceramics, and see why the anion matters as much as the lithium."],[8,"f","f7-1","Figure 8.1","The cast: two solvents, one anion, one lithium ion. Try this: drag the molecule round until the ringed oxygen points straight at you."],[8,"p","f7-1","figure 8.1","Let us begin with the cast. Everything in this module is made of four kinds of object, so we meet them first. A molecule is a group of atoms held together by chemical bonds; the figure draws each atom as a ball and each bond as a stick. The two solvents of a lithium-ion cell are both carbonates, built round a carbon atom bonded to three oxygen atoms. In ethylene carbonate, EC, the carbonate closes into a ring; in dimethyl carbonate, DMC, it stays an open chain: EC is a cyclic carbonate and DMC a linear one."],[8,"p","f7-1","figure 8.1","In both, one oxygen is double-bonded to the carbon and sticks out of the molecule: the carbonyl oxygen. A lithium ion is held by a solvent through the spare pairs of electrons on such a donor atom. The salt brings the other two objects: the lithium ion, Li+, a lithium atom that has lost one electron, and the hexafluorophosphate anion, PF6−, six fluorine atoms around a phosphorus atom, carrying one negative charge."],[8,"s","s-electron-clouds-and-charge","Electron clouds and charge",""],[8,"p","s-electron-clouds-and-charge","Electron clouds and charge","A molecule is not a set of hard balls. Its electrons form a cloud, densest at the atoms and thinning out over a fraction of a nanometre, and the figure shows that cloud as calculated for this page. The calculation also shows where each molecule is slightly negative and where slightly positive. EC has a negative end, at the carbonyl oxygen, and a positive end, at its CH2 groups: a strongly polar molecule, with a computed dipole moment of about 5 debye. In DMC the two halves almost cancel, about 0.3 debye. The same contrast shows up in the two liquids’ dielectric constants, 90 for EC against 3.1 for DMC, in the next figure, and simulations likewise treat EC as the polar solvent and DMC as the less polar one."],[8,"p","s-electron-clouds-and-charge","Electron clouds and charge","Four objects to recognize: EC, a ring; DMC, a chain; PF6−, a ball of fluorine; and Li+, the smallest of all."],[8,"f","f7-2","Figure 8.2","What the liquid is made of, counted molecule by molecule. Try this: switch to DMC alone and watch where the anions go."],[8,"s","s-the-recipe","The recipe",""],[8,"p","s-the-recipe","The recipe","Now we put the cast together. An electrolyte has one job that defines it: to provide purely ionic conduction between the two electrodes. It is a solution: a salt dissolved in a liquid, the solvent. Most commercial lithium-ion cells use roughly 1 mol of the salt lithium hexafluorophosphate, LiPF6, per litre of a mixture of carbonate solvents: a cyclic one such as ethylene carbonate (EC) or propylene carbonate (PC), and a linear one such as dimethyl carbonate (DMC), ethyl methyl carbonate (EMC) or diethyl carbonate (DEC)."],[8,"p","s-the-recipe","The recipe","Water, the obvious solvent, is out: it would be split at the voltage of a lithium cell (chapter 3). Water-based electrolytes conduct best, about 1 S/cm; the organic liquids reach 10−2 to 10−3 S/cm, because they dissolve salts less well and let the ions pair up."],[8,"s","s-why-two-solvents","Why two solvents",""],[8,"p","s-why-two-solvents","Why two solvents","Why two solvents? Because each does one job well and the other badly. A good solvent needs a high dielectric constant (relative permittivity), to pull the salt apart into separate ions, and a high fluidity, so that the ions can move. EC has a relative permittivity of 90 but is thick, 1.9 mPa s, and melts at about 36 °C, so on its own it is a solid at room temperature. DMC flows easily, 0.59 mPa s, but its relative permittivity is only 3.1."],[8,"p","s-why-two-solvents","Why two solvents","Mixed, they stay liquid far lower: a mixture of carbonates has a eutectic, the composition with the lowest melting point, at −8.6 °C for EC with DMC and −64.7 °C for EC with DMC and EMC (the solvents alone, without salt), and blends near the eutectic are preferred to work in the cold. EC is usually kept below 50 % by volume, preferably 30 %, because more of it comes out as a solid at low temperature."],[8,"s","s-a-pinch-of-additives","A pinch of additives",""],[8,"p","s-a-pinch-of-additives","A pinch of additives","Real electrolytes also carry minute amounts of additives, each with a job: some decompose on purpose to build a thin, controlled SEI on the graphite (chapter 10), others protect the positive electrode or stop overcharge (chapter 17); no single additive can do everything, and finding the right combination is now central to electrolyte work."],[8,"s","s-why-lipf","Why LiPF6",""],[8,"p","s-why-lipf","Why LiPF6","Why LiPF6? It is used almost exclusively in commercial lithium-ion cells because it has shown the best balance of the properties a salt needs: its solutions are among the most conductive known, it forms a stable surface on the aluminium current collector at high potential, and with carbonate solvents it forms a stable SEI on graphite (chapter 10). Its weakness is its P–F bond, which is easily broken: the salt reacts readily with water and is not very stable to heat, and hydrofluoric acid, HF, in LiPF6 electrolytes is one of the principal concerns about using it. It became the standard only when carbon replaced lithium metal and the solvents were optimized for the lithium-ion cells Sony introduced in 1991."],[8,"p","s-why-lipf","Why LiPF6","The liquid in a lithium-ion cell is mostly solvent: a dozen or so carbonate molecules for every lithium ion, two kinds of solvent because neither does both jobs."],[8,"f","f7-3","Figure 8.3","The ingredients of commercial electrolytes, side by side. Try this: in Salts, tap the aluminium symbol for LiTFSI; then in Solvents, compare the flash points of EC and DMC."],[8,"s","s-choosing-a-salt","Choosing a salt",""],[8,"p","s-choosing-a-salt","Choosing a salt","We have taken one salt and two solvents as given. Why these, and not others? Let us line up the alternatives, starting with the salt. A salt for a lithium-ion electrolyte has a list of jobs. It must give a conductive solution, stay stable within the cell’s voltage window and at high temperature, help form a good SEI, protect the aluminium current collector of the positive electrode above about 3.6 V vs Li, and resist water. It should also be cheap and not toxic. Failing any one of these rules a salt out of practical use, and every one of them depends on the rest of the recipe."],[8,"p","s-choosing-a-salt","Choosing a salt","Put the candidates side by side and no salt wins everywhere. LiClO4 conducts well and is stable, but its anion is a strong oxidant, which makes the salt a potential explosive. LiAsF6 behaves much like LiPF6, but reduction could turn its arsenic into highly toxic forms. LiBF4 resists water and heat better than LiPF6 but conducts significantly less."],[8,"p","s-choosing-a-salt","Choosing a salt","LiTFSI and lithium triflate shrug off water and heat, but in ordinary dilute solutions they corrode the aluminium foil at high potential. In PC:DME the aluminium repassivation potential, above which corrosion pits keep growing, is 3.7 V vs Li with LiTFSI, against above 5 V with LiPF6. So LiPF6, with its known weaknesses, keeps its place in the recipe of figure 7.2."],[8,"s","s-choosing-the-solvents","Choosing the solvents",""],[8,"p","s-choosing-the-solvents","Choosing the solvents","The solvents tell the same story. The five carbonates of commercial electrolytes split into two families. The cyclic ones, EC and PC, have relative permittivities of 65 to 90 and flash points above 130 °C, but they are viscous, and EC is a solid below 36 °C. The linear ones, DMC, EMC and DEC, flow two and a half to four times more easily and, apart from DMC, stay liquid far below 0 °C. But their permittivities are only about 3, and they flash at 17 to 25 °C, near room temperature."],[8,"p","s-choosing-the-solvents","Choosing the solvents","All five resist oxidation on glassy carbon to above 6 V vs Li. A window measured on inert carbon, however, is not a clear guide to stability against real electrode materials."],[8,"p","s-choosing-the-solvents","Choosing the solvents","Every salt and every solvent fails at something; a commercial electrolyte is the mixture whose weaknesses matter least."],[8,"f","f7-4","Figure 8.4","Dissolving the salt: the solvent takes the lithium ions one by one. Try this: follow one lithium ion from the edge of the solid until it is out in the liquid."],[8,"p","f7-4","figure 8.4","We have the recipe; now let us watch the salt go into the liquid. What does it mean for a salt to dissolve? Solid LiPF6 is made of Li+ cations and PF6− anions, which attract each other because their charges are opposite (chapter 0). In a carbonate solvent the Li+ ions are pulled out of it by the solvent molecules: each solvent molecule has an oxygen atom with spare pairs of electrons (in a carbonate, the carbonyl oxygen), and it binds to the lithium through them. The anions are, in general, left bare, because these solvents have no acidic hydrogen to hold them: in water an anion is held by hydrogen bonds, in a carbonate it is not. The anions can bind to Li+ in the same way, so solvent and anion compete for the places around each lithium ion, and that competition decides what is actually present in the liquid."],[8,"s","s-and-the-anion","And the anion?",""],[8,"p","s-and-the-anion","And the anion?","So does a PF6− anion get a shell of its own? Not in the way Li+ does. Solvent molecules do come close to it, and simulations treat a PF6− next to an EC molecule as a complex, but nothing binds there the way the oxygens bind to lithium, and the neighbours keep changing. In the figure they are drawn faint, with their slightly positive CH ends (blue in figure 8.1) towards the anion."],[8,"p","s-and-the-anion","And the anion?","Dissolving is a tug of war for the lithium ion, and in a good solvent the solvent wins."],[8,"f","f7-5","Figure 8.5","One lithium ion and its shell, in three dimensions. Try this: drag the shell round until you can see all four molecules, then watch one pull away and come back."],[8,"s","s-the-shell","The shell",""],[8,"p","s-the-shell","The shell","Let us follow one lithium ion out of the crystal and look at what it has gathered. How many solvent molecules fit round it? Few. Simulations find about four solvent molecules bound strongly around a lithium ion, arranged as a tetrahedron, and about 4.2 to 4.4 partners, solvent and anions together, in EC : DMC and EC : PC with LiPF6; neutron diffraction on LiPF6 in PC finds 4.5 PC molecules on average, each oxygen 0.204 nm from the lithium."],[8,"p","s-the-shell","The shell","This group is the ion’s first solvation shell. In the liquid, simulations find EC and DMC in it about equally often, even though an isolated lithium ion in the gas phase prefers EC. The shell is not a fixed object: a given EC molecule stays in it for about half a nanosecond, an anion partner for 2 to 3 ns."],[8,"s","s-pairs-and-clusters","Pairs and clusters",""],[8,"p","s-pairs-and-clusters","Pairs and clusters","When an anion wins a place, the two ions form a contact ion pair; when solvent stays between them, they are a solvent-separated ion pair; larger clusters are aggregates. The anion matters: the tendency to pair up rises in the order LiAsF6 and LiPF6, then LiTFSI, LiClO4, LiBF4 and lithium triflate."],[8,"p","s-pairs-and-clusters","Pairs and clusters","The solvent matters too: in simulations of EC : DMC the fraction of free ions rises nearly linearly with the share of EC; and measured with LiTFSI, the conductivity reaches 62 % of what fully independent ions would give in PC, but only 11 % in DMC. Not every cluster is lost to conduction: charged clusters carry current too."],[8,"p","s-pairs-and-clusters","Pairs and clusters","A lithium ion never travels alone: it carries a shell of about four solvent molecules, and sometimes an anion."],[8,"p","s-pairs-and-clusters","Pairs and clusters","You can now explain what the liquid in a lithium-ion cell is made of and why it mixes two solvents, what a solvation shell is and how ions pair up, how an ion wanders and drifts and how weak the field in the bulk is, how the liquid changes layer by layer towards an electrode, how a liquid, a polymer and a ceramic each carry a lithium ion, why dry-polymer cells run warm, what the transference number is and why a low one leaves a salt gradient, and why a solid electrolyte needs more than a high conductivity."],[8,"p","s-pairs-and-clusters","Pairs and clusters","Our ion has now crossed the electrolyte. On the far side, it has to find a particle."],[9,"p","","","How does an ion cross the cell, and what does it meet at the electrode?"],[9,"f","f7-6","Figure 9.1","How an ion moves: a random walk, gently steered. Try this: switch the field on and off and compare the tracks."],[9,"s","s-wandering","Wandering",""],[9,"p","s-wandering","Wandering","So far our ion has sat in its shell; now we set it moving. Even with no current, nothing in the liquid is still. Every ion is bumped continually by the molecules around it and wanders at random, in every direction alike. On average it goes nowhere; it only spreads out, a distance that grows as the square root of time, (2Dt)1/2, where D is its diffusion coefficient. This is diffusion, met inside the electrode particles in chapter 7."],[9,"s","s-drifting-in-a-field","Drifting in a field",""],[9,"p","s-drifting-in-a-field","Drifting in a field","Draw current and a small electric field appears across the liquid. It does not replace the random wandering; it biases it: each step is pushed slightly one way, cations down the potential and anions up it. That steady bias is migration, and the Nernst-Planck equation adds the two together (chapter 0)."],[9,"p","s-drifting-in-a-field","Drifting in a field","What moves is the ion together with whatever stays bound to it, which is why a bigger moving object, or a thicker liquid, conducts less: u = |z|e/(6πηr). The lithium ion moves in two ways: it carries its first shell along with it, or it is handed on, leaving some solvent molecules behind and gaining others. In EC : DMC with LiPF6 the two contribute roughly equally; in short ethylene-oxide chains (oligoethers) the ion travels a distance of three to four solvent molecules before its shell is renewed, so there carrying dominates."],[9,"p","s-drifting-in-a-field","Drifting in a field","A picture to keep: ionic conduction is a crowd crossing a corridor. More people, or people who move more freely, carry more across per second. The picture breaks in two places: the ions also push on each other through the field, and in a polymer the corridor itself has to keep moving for anyone to get through."],[9,"p","s-drifting-in-a-field","Drifting in a field","An ion in a liquid wanders at random; a current only nudges the wandering, gently and all the same way."],[9,"f","f7-10","Figure 9.2","The transference number, and the salt gradient it leaves behind. Try this: slide t+ from 0.3 to 0.6 and watch the gradient shrink by almost half; then all the way to 1."],[9,"p","f7-10","figure 9.2","Conductivity is not the whole story, because both ions move but only one is wanted. The transference number t+ is the fraction of the ionic conductivity carried by Li+; in a dilute electrolyte it is the ratio of the lithium diffusion coefficient to the sum of both. (It is also called the transport number.) In the organic liquid electrolytes of 1994 it was typically below 0.5."],[9,"p","f7-10","figure 9.2","The rest of the current is carried by anions that no electrode can take in, so in a working cell salt builds up at one electrode and runs short at the other, and the steady-state difference grows with (1 − t+)."],[9,"p","f7-10","figure 9.2","That gradient is the concentration polarization of chapter 5, seen from the electrolyte. In polymer electrolytes t+ is about 0.3; about 10 % of ceramic nanofillers lifts it to about 0.6, raises the conductivity severalfold at 60 to 80 °C, and gives a stable interface with lithium."],[9,"p","f7-10","figure 9.2","The logical end point is a single-ion conductor, in which the anions are tied to the polymer so that t+ approaches 1 and no salt gradient builds up; one block copolymer of this kind reached t+ above 0.85, though at only 1.3 × 10−5 S/cm at 60 °C, and conducting 10−4 S/cm at room temperature remains the challenge."],[9,"f","f7-7","Figure 9.3","Layer by layer, from the middle of the liquid to the electrode. Try this: switch from Negative to Positive and watch which way the red oxygens point in the first layer."],[9,"s","s-the-bulk-and-the-diffuse-layer","The bulk and the diffuse layer",""],[9,"p","s-the-bulk-and-the-diffuse-layer","The bulk and the diffuse layer","We have followed the ion through the middle of the liquid. Now let us walk towards an electrode, because the electrolyte is not the same all the way across. Far from either electrode, in the bulk, every small volume holds as much positive as negative charge: a separation of charge there would set up a field large enough to erase it at once. The molecules point every way, and with no current the potential is flat. With current, the only field is the gentle ohmic one of the worked example above, about 0.1 V/cm."],[9,"p","s-the-bulk-and-the-diffuse-layer","The bulk and the diffuse layer","A few nanometres from a charged electrode this changes. Ions of the opposite sign crowd towards the surface and solvent molecules turn: together they cancel the electrode’s charge, so its field does not reach the bulk. The excess of ions thins out over the diffuse layer, less than about 10 nm thick above 0.01 M; closest to the surface is the compact layer, one solvent shell thick."],[9,"p","s-the-bulk-and-the-diffuse-layer","The bulk and the diffuse layer","The field inside that thin step can reach 107 V/cm, a hundred million times the field in the bulk of the worked example. This is the electrical double layer of chapter 3, now seen molecule by molecule."],[9,"s","s-the-first-molecular-layer","The first molecular layer",""],[9,"p","s-the-first-molecular-layer","The first molecular layer","Closer still, we reach single molecules. Simulations show what the innermost layer looks like in EC : DMC with LiPF6. When the electrode is charged, either way, the more polar EC replaces DMC at the surface, an effect confirmed by experiment on LiCoO2. At a negative electrode the carbonyl groups are pushed away from the surface, and at the most negative potentials lithium ions sit against it, solvated mainly by EC, which fits the observation that EC reduction dominates the SEI formed in these mixtures. At a positive electrode the layer fills with carbonyl groups and PF6− anions accumulate, where they are expected to take part in oxidation reactions."],[9,"s","s-getting-in","Getting in",""],[9,"p","s-getting-in","Getting in","One step remains: our ion must get into the electrode. To enter graphite a lithium ion has to leave its shell. In preliminary simulations of bare graphite, with no SEI, a lithium ion rests about 0.5 nm from the surface, still solvated; closer than about 0.4 nm the free energy climbs steeply as it is forced to shed its solvent, consistent with DMC going first and EC last. The size of that climb agrees with measured activation energies of 16.4 to 17 kcal/mol, about 70 kJ/mol. The two contributions usually discussed for the resistance of this interface are the SEI and this desolvation, and experiments show that at low temperature the interface can resist more than the whole bulk electrolyte."],[9,"p","s-getting-in","Getting in","Uniform in the middle, ordered in the last nanometre: the electrolyte’s structure, and almost all of its field, change within a few molecules of the electrode."],[9,"p","s-getting-in","Getting in","The conductivity of an electrolyte is the sum of what each ion contributes: κ = F Σ|zj|ujCj, with uj the mobility and Cj the concentration of ion j. The share carried by one ion is its transference number, tj = |zj|ujCj / Σ|zk|ukCk. For a salt of one cation and one anion this reduces to t+ = |z+|u+/(|z+|u+ + |z−|u−), and in the dilute limit to DLi/(DLi + D−). Chapter 0 gave the mobility of a sphere in a viscous liquid, u = |z|e/(6πηr): a bigger ion, or a more viscous liquid, conducts less."],[9,"p","s-getting-in","Getting in","How independent are the ions? Measure each ion’s self-diffusion coefficient, D+ and D−, and compute the conductivity the ions would give if each moved on its own, λNE = e2(n+D+ + n−D−)/(VkBT), for n ions of each kind in a volume V. The measured conductivity divided by it is the ionicity, αd = λ/λNE: 0.62 for LiTFSI in PC and 0.11 in DMC, both at 20 solvent molecules per lithium and 298 K. Why about 1 mol per litre in the first place? Enough salt has to dissolve to provide plenty of charge carriers, and the conductivity is frequently found to peak near 1 M."],[9,"p","s-getting-in","Getting in","Even water can be stretched. Water splits over a window of 1.23 V (chapter 3), but in “water-in-salt” electrolytes, with far more salt than water by weight, the salt crowds the electrode surfaces and its decomposition builds a LiF-rich interphase; with further measures to keep water off the negative electrode, aqueous electrolytes have reached stability windows above 4 V."],[10,"p","","","What is the solid electrolyte interphase, and why does every cell need one?"],[10,"f","f6-3","Figure 10.1","The first charge, and the layer that costs lithium mostly once and then protects. Try this: watch the black bar on the right; nothing happens at the surface until it crosses the green edge."],[10,"s","s-the-first-charge","The first charge",""],[10,"p","s-the-first-charge","The first charge","So far we have taken the hosts as we find them in a working cell. But a cell has a beginning, and the beginning leaves a mark. A lithium-ion cell is assembled discharged, with all its lithium in the positive electrode. On the first charge the graphite is driven down to about 0.2 V vs Li/Li+, below the lower edge of the window in which the carbonate electrolyte is stable (chapter 3). The electrolyte is reduced at the graphite surface, and its products form a passivating layer, the solid-electrolyte interphase or SEI. Forming it consumes lithium from the positive electrode irreversibly, and the layer adds to the interfacial impedance; it also changes as the cell cycles."],[10,"p","s-the-first-charge","The first charge","What is reduced, and what does the layer contain? Among the solvents, ethylene carbonate is reduced first, from about 0.8 V vs Li/Li+ (with LiPF6 the anion starts earlier; see Go deeper). Two EC molecules, two lithium ions and two electrons give lithium ethylene dicarbonate (Li2EDC) and ethylene gas; the layer that forms has a dense inorganic inner part next to the graphite (Li2CO3, LiF, Li2O) and a porous organic outer part."],[10,"p","s-the-first-charge","The first charge","How thick does it get? A crystalline layer only 2 to 3 nm thick would already stop electrons tunnelling through, but on graphite the SEI soon grows past 10 nm and reaches about 50 nm after cycling."],[10,"s","s-why-the-layer-helps","Why the layer helps",""],[10,"p","s-why-the-layer-helps","Why the layer helps","We have paid lithium for this layer; let us see what we get back. What makes the SEI useful is its selectivity: it is electronically insulating, so it stops further reduction, yet it conducts Li+, so the graphite keeps filling. Of the main solvents, only ethylene carbonate forms a protective layer on graphite, which is why it is in almost every commercial electrolyte; propylene carbonate does not, and as late as 2001 the reason was still described as unclear."],[10,"p","s-why-the-layer-helps","Why the layer helps","Why the difference? The explanation usually given, which goes back to Dahn and co-workers in 1990, is that EC decomposes first and builds an SEI that stops PC from slipping into the graphite together with the lithium; PC holds on to its Li+ more strongly, goes into the graphite with it, and peels the graphite layers apart."],[10,"p","s-why-the-layer-helps","Why the layer helps","By 1994 the picture could be put simply: the layer forms during the first insertion, and after it intercalation is completely reversible. In chapter 16 we shall see that this is only nearly true. Conversely, an electrode that works inside the window needs no SEI. Lithium titanate at 1.5 V forms none, and in one report electrodes of lithium titanate and LiFePO4 survived 30 000 cycles at 5C."],[10,"s","s-steering-the-sei-on-purpose","Steering the SEI on purpose",""],[10,"p","s-steering-the-sei-on-purpose","Steering the SEI on purpose","If the layer matters this much, we want to control it. Today the SEI is also steered on purpose, with small amounts of additives that decompose first and leave a thin, controlled layer. The best known is vinylene carbonate (VC): reduced at the graphite above 1 V vs Li/Li+, it forms a passivation layer, and it suppresses the decomposition of PC."],[10,"p","s-steering-the-sei-on-purpose","Steering the SEI on purpose","Why new cells are “formed”. Because the SEI decides how the cell will age, a factory does not leave its growth to chance: near the end of making a cell, it is cycled slowly under precisely defined conditions, for up to a day, and let the gas out before the cell is sealed for good. It is expensive, about a third (32 %) of the cost of making a cell, mostly for keeping cells warm while they condition. And the first cycle is where the big loss happens: in one recent laboratory cell the first cycle returned about 82.5 % of the charge put in, the second about 97.5 %."],[10,"p","s-steering-the-sei-on-purpose","Steering the SEI on purpose","Not only on graphite. A positive electrode pushed above the upper edge of the window, about 4.3 V, oxidizes the electrolyte instead; LiNi0.5Mn1.5O4 at about 4.75 V is protected by a passivation layer of its own, also called an SEI."],[10,"p","s-steering-the-sei-on-purpose","Steering the SEI on purpose","The SEI costs most of its lithium on the first charge, and then lets the cell work outside its electrolyte’s window."],[10,"f","f9-3","Figure 10.2","Graphite’s skin: the SEI, and the ways a graphite electrode ages. Try this: step through the three callouts, then slide the temperature from 25 °C to 55 °C and down to −10 °C."],[10,"s","s-graphite-still-the-workhorse","Graphite, still the workhorse",""],[10,"p","s-graphite-still-the-workhorse","Graphite, still the workhorse","Of the three mechanisms, intercalation is the one we met in chapter 7, so let us look at graphite first. Sony’s first lithium-ion cell of 1991 paired graphite with LiCoO2, and three decades later graphite, alone or with some silicon, is still the negative electrode of high-energy cells, now facing nickel-rich layered oxides. It is cheap, conducts electrons well, and lithium goes in at about 0.15 V with only 10 to 50 mV of hysteresis."],[10,"p","s-graphite-still-the-workhorse","Graphite, still the workhorse","Its one real weakness is that it works below the potential at which the electrolyte is stable. On the first charge the electrolyte is reduced on its surface and leaves the SEI (chapter 10), consuming lithium; once the layer is complete it protects the graphite and lets it cycle. Changes at this interface are a major cause of ageing in commercial cells, through five routes: exfoliation and cracking of the graphite, an SEI that keeps forming and dissolving, SEI conversion and growth, SEI dissolution, and lithium plating. Graphite particles also crack along their grain boundaries as they expand and shrink, exposing new surface that builds more SEI."],[10,"p","s-graphite-still-the-workhorse","Graphite, still the workhorse","Temperature decides which route dominates: hot, the SEI grows thicker and consumes lithium and electrolyte; cold, lithium plates on the graphite instead of entering it. We follow the growth of the SEI over time in chapter 16."],[10,"p","s-graphite-still-the-workhorse","Graphite, still the workhorse","Where graphite comes from. Natural graphite costs about $8000 to $11 000 per tonne, synthetic graphite about $20 000; China produces nearly 75 % of the world’s graphite, which is why the United States and Europe are building their own supply. Demand is expected to reach about 1800 kt by 2030."],[10,"p","s-graphite-still-the-workhorse","Graphite, still the workhorse","We can draw the electrolyte as having its own window: an energy gap between its lowest unoccupied and highest occupied molecular orbitals. A negative electrode whose electrochemical potential μA lies above the lowest unoccupied orbital reduces the electrolyte; a positive electrode whose μC lies below the highest occupied orbital oxidizes it, unless a passivating layer blocks the electron transfer. For the carbonate electrolytes the lowest unoccupied orbital sits about 1.1 eV below the lithium level and the practical limit at the top is about 4.3 eV below it, which on the voltage scale of chapter 3 is the window from about 1.1 to about 4.3 V vs Li/Li+. Graphite, at about 0.2 V, is outside it, and so works only behind its SEI."],[10,"p","s-graphite-still-the-workhorse","Graphite, still the workhorse","The orbital picture is a simplification. Real reduction and oxidation potentials depend on the salt and on the molecules around each one: estimates from isolated molecules can be far off: oxidizing EC next to PF6−, with a hydrogen passed to a neighbour, takes almost 2 V less than oxidizing an isolated EC molecule, and in an EC/EMC electrolyte reduction is measured to start at about 1.7 V with LiPF6 but about 0.8 V with LiBF4. Most solvents and salt anions are thermodynamically unstable where lithium inserts into graphite; the electrolyte survives there only kinetically, behind the insulating interphase it has built."],[10,"p","s-graphite-still-the-workhorse","Graphite, still the workhorse","What a good SEI has to do comes down to four things: block electrons, so that no more electrolyte is reduced; let Li+ through without much hindrance; stay chemically stable, neither reacting with nor dissolving into the electrolyte; and stay mechanically intact while the electrode under it swells and shrinks. Peled introduced the idea of an electronically insulating, ionically conducting interphase in 1979."],[11,"p","","","Why is an electrode a porous coating of particles, carbon and binder?"],[11,"p","","","Where we are. In chapter 9 we carried our ion across the electrolyte. On the far side it has to get into a particle of the positive electrode, one of the two hosts of chapter 7. In chapter 2 we saw that an electrode is not a solid block of that host but a coating of particles, carbon, binder and pores. In this module we ask what each of those ingredients is for, and what happens when one of them fails."],[11,"f","f8-1","Figure 11.1","Two roads to every particle: electrons through the carbon, ions through the pores. Try this: break carbon contacts several times and look for a particle that goes dark while its own links look intact: what counts is an unbroken path all the way to the foil."],[11,"p","f8-1","figure 11.1","Let us look at the coating our ion is about to enter. Most battery electrodes are porous composites: particles of the active material, a conductive diluent such as carbon or a metal powder, and a polymer binder, coated on a current collector. About 30 % of the coating is pore space, so the surface where the reaction can happen is far larger than the electrode’s geometric area. A large surface lowers the current density at each point of it, and with it the polarization."],[11,"p","f8-1","figure 11.1","Each ingredient, then, keeps one road open. The carbon carries electrons from the foil to every particle; the electrolyte in the pores carries ions from the separator to every particle; the binder holds the coating together and onto the foil. A particle that one of the two roads cannot reach stores nothing and only adds mass."],[11,"p","f8-1","figure 11.1","A picture to keep: a town where every house needs two roads, one for each of two deliveries that must arrive together, and is only lived in if both reach it. The picture breaks because here the roads also have tolls: the farther a delivery travels through the coating, the more voltage it costs."],[11,"p","f8-1","figure 11.1","A particle that the electrons or the ions cannot reach contributes nothing."],[11,"f","f8-2","Figure 11.2","Where does the current go inside an electrode? Try this: set the two conductivities equal and the thickness to 0.2; then thicken the electrode and watch the middle go quiet."],[11,"p","f8-2","figure 11.2","The tolls decide where in the coating the work gets done. Inside a porous electrode the reaction is not spread evenly. Where it happens depends on the conductivities of the solid matrix and of the electrolyte, the exchange current, diffusion, the thickness of the electrode, its porosity, pore size and tortuosity; and a nonuniform current distribution wastes active material."],[11,"p","f8-2","figure 11.2","The reaction concentrates where the electrons and the ions together have the shortest, cheapest way to meet. In the simplest theory the profile is a hyperbolic cosine with a characteristic depth. This reaction-limited picture is most realistic for thin electrodes, of order 10 μm, and needs corrections beyond about 100 μm."],[11,"p","f8-2","figure 11.2","Part of the toll is paid in the pores themselves. The electrolyte inside the pores conducts worse than in bulk, because the ions have less room and a winding path. The usual correction is κeff = κεα, with ε the porosity and α = 1.5 (1.53 for LiFePO4 electrodes). Ionic transport is far slower than electronic transport, which is also why cells use thin electrodes of large area."],[11,"f","f8-3","Figure 11.3","A poor conductor needs carbon. Try this: switch the right-hand particle to a carbon coating and compare how long each one takes to light up fully."],[11,"p","f8-3","figure 11.3","So far we have assumed that once electrons reach a particle, they reach all of it. For many active materials that is not so. An active material that conducts electrons poorly works only where it touches the conductive additive, and the reacted zone spreads outward from that contact, as in MnO2. LiFePO4 is the modern case: its poor electronic conductivity was overcome by coating the particles with carbon and making them small."],[11,"p","f8-3","figure 11.3","The coating, introduced by Armand and co-workers, is made by decomposing a polymer precursor on the particles, which leaves a uniform layer of carbon. With it, a material that was once a dead end is now produced in thousands of tonnes a year."],[11,"f","f8-4","Figure 11.4","The nanoparticle trade-off: faster particles, emptier litres. Try this: shrink the particles to a tenth and read both bars."],[11,"p","f8-4","figure 11.4","The other half of that remedy, making the particles small, has its own price. Small particles bring the ions closer to every atom: the time to cross a particle falls with the square of its size (chapter 7). But carbon-coated nanoparticles have a lower tap density, so the electrode holds less energy per litre, and coating them evenly gets harder as they shrink. Highly porous aerogel electrodes pay the same price, at a tap density of about 0.2 g/cm3."],[11,"f","f5-8p","Figure 11.5","Porosity and tortuosity: how much room the ions have, and how far round they must go. Try this: at porosity 0.30, switch from round particles to flat plates and compare the black route; then slide the porosity down and watch every curve on the right fall."],[11,"p","f5-8p","figure 11.5","So far we have followed the electrons to each particle. The ions must reach the particles too, and for that we must look at the space between them. An electrode is not a solid block. It is a composite: particles of active material, often wired together by a conductive additive such as carbon, held by a binder, with about 30 % of its volume left as pores that the electrolyte fills. Every ion that reaches a particle has travelled through those pores, and two numbers describe them."],[11,"s","s-porosity-how-much-room","Porosity: how much room",""],[11,"p","s-porosity-how-much-room","Porosity: how much room","The porosity ε is the fraction of the electrode’s volume that is pore. It is well defined and easy to find. We weigh a disc of the coating and measure its thickness. From the masses and densities of the solid ingredients we get the volume the solids fill, and whatever is left of the coating’s volume is pore."],[11,"p","s-porosity-how-much-room","Porosity: how much room","Freshly coated electrodes are then pressed, or calendered, to lower their porosity. That packs more energy into each litre and improves the electronic contact through the coating and to the current collector; typical lithium-ion electrodes end at roughly 30 %."],[11,"s","s-tortuosity-how-far-round","Tortuosity: how far round",""],[11,"p","s-tortuosity-how-far-round","Tortuosity: how far round","The pores do not run straight across. An ion has to wind round the particles, so its path is longer than the coating is thick, and the channels narrow and widen on the way. The tortuosity τ collects these effects. In its simplest, geometric sense it is the length of the path divided by the straight distance. The tortuosity that matters for transport, the effective tortuosity, also includes the changing cross-section and the rough surfaces; it is found by measurement, or by simulation on a three-dimensional image of the electrode."],[11,"p","s-tortuosity-how-far-round","Tortuosity: how far round","Together the two numbers set how well the electrolyte conducts inside the coating, κeff=κε/τ . The ratio NM=κ/κeff=τ/ε , the MacMullin number, says how many times worse the filled pores conduct than the free liquid. Battery models often take the tortuosity from the porosity alone, with the Bruggeman relation τ=ε−0.5 , which gives κeff=κε1.5 . The modelled cell in the next figure uses exactly this correction, for diffusion as well as for conduction."],[11,"s","s-measuring-it-and-why-it-matters","Measuring it, and why it matters",""],[11,"p","s-measuring-it-and-why-it-matters","Measuring it, and why it matters","How is τ measured? One clean way builds a cell from two identical electrodes and fills it with a salt that cannot react with them or enter the particles, so that no charge crosses into the particles and the ions can only charge the pore walls. The impedance spectrum then follows a transmission line, a ladder of resistances along the pores and capacitances at their walls that we meet again in Lab 3. Fitting it gives the ionic resistance Rion of the pores, and for the pair of electrodes τ=RionAκε/(2d) , with A the area and d the thickness of one electrode."],[11,"p","s-measuring-it-and-why-it-matters","Measuring it, and why it matters","Measured this way, real electrodes are worse than the estimate. In one systematic study, every electrode tested had a MacMullin number larger than the Bruggeman relation predicts, by about 1.5 to 3 times in the authors’ summary and by more in some of their fits (figure 11.5). The measured curves fall with porosity at roughly the same power as the estimate, exponents of 1.1 to 1.6 against 1.5, but start three to five times lower. A graphite electrode of porosity 0.43 gave τ=4.3±0.6 , about three times the estimate of 1.5 (the fitted graphite curve in figure 11.5, drawn through electrodes of many porosities, runs a little lower than this single point). Graphite’s plate-like particles lie flat in the coating and hinder the way across: at similar porosities, electrodes of round NMC particles had MacMullin numbers of 10 to 11, against 18 to 19 for graphite. Particle size and the amount of conductive carbon matter too."],[11,"p","s-measuring-it-and-why-it-matters","Measuring it, and why it matters","Porosity, from a balance and a thickness gauge."],[11,"p","s-measuring-it-and-why-it-matters","Measuring it, and why it matters","This is why the two numbers matter. At high rates the performance of a cell is largely set by the ionic resistance of its electrodes and separator, filled with electrolyte, and porosity and tortuosity are the key to that resistance. Pressing a coating therefore cuts both ways: more material in each litre, but less room for the ions and a longer way round. The next figure shows what happens when the ions run short."],[11,"p","s-measuring-it-and-why-it-matters","Measuring it, and why it matters","Porosity says how much room the ions have, tortuosity how far round they must go; together they divide the electrolyte’s conductivity inside the coating."],[11,"f","f5-8","Figure 11.6","Inside a porous electrode: why fast discharges stop early. Try this: switch from 10 to 20 A/m2 and watch the blue fill stay near the separator while the salt at the back runs out."],[11,"p","f5-8","figure 11.6","Now let us run a discharge through such a coating and look across its whole thickness. One model treats a composite of TiS2 particles, themselves good electronic conductors, mixed with a polymer electrolyte, as two continua, the particles and the electrolyte, laid over each other. The modelled cell had a lithium foil, the same polymer electrolyte as separator and this TiS2 positive electrode."],[11,"p","f5-8","figure 11.6","The computed discharges show the main lesson at once. The faster the current, the less of the material is used before the voltage collapses: at 20 A/m2 the voltage drops sharply when only about 30 % of the positive material has reacted."],[11,"s","s-where-the-reaction-happens","Where the reaction happens",""],[11,"p","s-where-the-reaction-happens","Where the reaction happens","Why does the material go unused? Let us follow where the reaction happens. At first it crowds near the separator. The particles carry electrons about 105 times better than the polymer carries ions, so the short ionic path wins. As the particles at the front fill up, the reaction moves towards the middle of the electrode."],[11,"p","s-where-the-reaction-happens","Where the reaction happens","Meanwhile the salt piles up near the lithium foil and is drawn down at the back of the electrode, where it cannot be replaced fast enough. There the conductivity falls and the material is poorly used."],[11,"p","s-where-the-reaction-happens","Where the reaction happens","Two dimensionless numbers decide which transport matters, each a ratio of a transport time to the discharge time. For diffusion inside the modelled particles, of 1 µm radius, it was only 0.0001, so solid diffusion hardly mattered: the particles would have had to be as large as the electrode is thick. For the electrolyte it was 0.15 at 10 A/m2, and at high currents it is the electrolyte that ends the discharge early."],[11,"p","s-where-the-reaction-happens","Where the reaction happens","The design lesson follows. Keeping the theoretical capacity fixed, a more open electrode (porosity 0.60 instead of 0.30, and so thicker; in figure 11.6, 175 µm instead of 100 µm) raised the material used at 10 A/m2 from 84 % to 97 %; 0.60 was the optimum found. In general, thinner electrodes use their material better when the electrolyte is the bottleneck, at the cost of harder processing and the extra weight of more, thinner cells. A stronger starting salt solution was also suggested, because the 1 M presumably chosen for its conductivity is far weaker by the time it reaches the depths of the electrode."],[11,"p","s-where-the-reaction-happens","Where the reaction happens","At high current the back of a thick electrode starves of ions, so the cell stops with material unused."],[11,"f","f8-5","Figure 11.7","From powder to cell in eight steps. Try this: step through and find the two steps where the pores of figure 11.1 are made and then partly squeezed out."],[11,"p","f8-5","figure 11.7","So far we have taken the coating as given. Let us see how it is made, because the making decides how well the two roads work. The composite of this module is made in a few industrial steps. The powders, about 90 % active material by mass with conductive carbon and a polymer binder, are mixed into a slurry, coated on the current-collector foil, dried so that the pores form, and pressed between rollers to an even, denser layer; the electrodes are then cut, stacked or wound with the separator, filled with electrolyte, and cycled for the first time to form the SEI."],[11,"p","f8-5","figure 11.7","Every step leaves its mark on the roads of figure 11.1: where the carbon and binder end up, how open the pores stay, how well the electrolyte wets them."],[11,"p","f8-5","figure 11.7","You can now explain what each ingredient of an electrode coating is for, why a particle without both roads is dead weight, where the reaction happens inside a thick electrode and why, why poorly conducting materials need carbon, and what small particles gain and cost."],[11,"p","f8-5","figure 11.7","Our ion now has a round trip that works: across the electrolyte, into a particle, and back. Now let us push the electrodes further, starting with the negative one, and see how the round trip fails."],[11,"p","f8-5","figure 11.7","Let us write the current in the solid i1 and in the electrolyte i2; their sum is the cell current, and the electrolyte current grows through the thickness by whatever the reaction converts, di2/dx = a j, with a the reacting area per volume. Ohm’s law in each phase, dφ1/dx = −i1/σ and dφ2/dx = −i2/κ, and the linearized kinetics of chapter 5, j = η/Rct, with η = φ1 − φ2 − U the local overpotential, give d2η/dx2 = (ν/L)2η, with ν2 = aL2(1/σ + 1/κ)/Rct, the same balance of current that underlies the porous-electrode equations. Its solution is the cosh profile of figure 11.2, and 1/ν is the reaction depth as a fraction of the thickness. This reaction-limited case and its depth scale have been analysed in full; when the depth is much larger than the electrode the overpotential is uniform."],[12,"p","","","What could replace graphite, and why has it not happened yet?"],[12,"p","","","Where we are. In chapter 11 we built real electrodes out of particles, carbon, binder and pores, and our ion made the round trip through them. The round trip works; now let us push it further. We begin with the negative electrode, the side that holds the lithium when your cell is charged: first graphite, which made the first lithium-ion cell possible, then the materials that might replace it, silicon, conversion materials and lithium metal itself, and the ions that might one day replace lithium. Then we cross to the positive electrode: the oxides in today’s cells, why nickel-rich NMC trades stability for capacity, what comes next, and the organic molecules that do without transition metals. Along the way we answer the question above."],[12,"f","f9-1","Figure 12.1","The map of negative electrodes, and what extra capacity really buys. Try this: compare silicon and lithium metal; then try Li4Ti5O12 and see what its voltage costs."],[12,"p","f9-1","figure 12.1","Let us start with what the negative electrode has to do. It stores the lithium while the cell is charged and gives it back on discharge. It was the negative electrode that made the first lithium-ion cell possible: graphite held lithium reversibly and stayed stable in carbonate electrolytes, where lithium metal did not. Graphite’s 372 mAh/g now limits how much energy a cell can hold, and the search for something better has produced four families of material, named for how they store lithium: intercalation hosts, alloys, conversion materials and lithium metal."],[12,"p","f9-1","figure 12.1","What would a better material have to offer? A good negative electrode needs a high reversible capacity, a low potential, fast kinetics, low cost, stability against the electrolyte, a stable SEI and a light environmental footprint. On the first two counts lithium metal and silicon stand out; both still face serious problems at the level of the material, the electrode and the cell."],[12,"p","f9-1","figure 12.1","A negative electrode should sit low and store a lot, but past a point the positive electrode sets the limit."],[12,"f","f9-4","Figure 12.2","Fast charging: how close can the negative electrode come to 0 V? Try this: at 25 °C find the rate at which plating starts; then cool the cell to 0 °C and find it again."],[12,"s","s-charging-fast","Charging fast",""],[12,"p","s-charging-fast","Charging fast","Plating, which we just met in the cold, is also a risk when we charge quickly. Electric cars, drones, electric aircraft and grid storage all want faster charging; industry aims to reach 80 % of the charge in under 15 minutes without losing cycle life or safety. On the negative electrode three things go wrong. Lithium can arrive faster than it can enter the graphite and plate as metal, a risk made worse by graphite’s low potential; the particles are stressed and crack; and the heat destabilizes the SEI."],[12,"p","s-charging-fast","Charging fast","The remedies run from material to cell: smaller particles and porous structures; thin, porous electrodes of low tortuosity; electrolytes with a LiFSI salt or an FEC additive, which improve the SEI and reduce plating; and, at cell level, the right ratio of negative to positive capacity (the N/P ratio), the charging protocol and cooling. Silicon-based negative electrodes are being developed for fast charging too: one company reports silicon nanowires charged from 0 to 80 % in under 6 minutes, another columnar silicon grown on copper charged to 80 % in 7 minutes at 1350 Wh/L."],[12,"p","s-charging-fast","Charging fast","The other route is to move away from 0 V. Lithium titanate, Li4Ti5O12, works at about 1.55 V, where little SEI forms, and changes volume by only about 0.2 %, a “zero-strain” material: it is safe, long-lived and fast, but stores only 175 mAh/g, conducts poorly and can give off gas (H2, CO2, CO) by decomposing the electrolyte on its surface, a mechanism not yet well understood. Niobium oxides such as Nb2O5, TiNb2O7 and Nb14W3O44 also work at 1 to 2 V, store more (about 200 to 350 mAh/g), change little in volume and charge very fast; they are already being made at industrial scale for high-power cells."],[12,"f","f9-5","Figure 12.3","Silicon breathes, and its SEI pays for it. Try this: watch the silicon for three cycles, then give it room to swell; slide the silicon fraction from 5 to 20 wt%."],[12,"s","s-silicon-ten-times-the-capacity-four-times-the-vo","Silicon: ten times the capacity, four times the volume",""],[12,"p","s-silicon-ten-times-the-capacity-four-times-the-vo","Silicon: ten times the capacity, four times the volume","Titanate trades energy for margin. Silicon makes the opposite trade. It stores 3579 mAh/g, close to ten times graphite, at a low potential, and it is cheap. Its problem is volume: it swells by about 280 % when fully lithiated. The particles pulverize, lose contact with each other, and the electrode can come away from its current collector; the SEI breaks on every swelling and forms again on the fresh surface, using up lithium and electrolyte, so the first-cycle efficiency is low and the capacity fades fast. Silicon also conducts electrons poorly."],[12,"p","s-silicon-ten-times-the-capacity-four-times-the-vo","Silicon: ten times the capacity, four times the volume","Nanoparticles, porous and hollow spheres, nanowires, yolk–shell and pomegranate-like structures all help, though small particles cost volumetric energy. Binders that hold the contact, perhaps self-healing polymers, could be the breakthrough. Today’s best cells already use graphite with 5 to 10 wt% silicon; more than 20 % is the industry’s next step, trading energy against cycle life. The market for silicon negative electrodes, about $279 million in 2023, is projected to reach $15 billion by 2035."],[12,"f","f9-6","Figure 12.4","Conversion: high capacity, paid for with a volt of hysteresis. Try this: halve the hysteresis and read how much of the energy comes back."],[12,"p","f9-6","figure 12.4","The third mechanism goes further still. A conversion electrode is not a host at all. Lithium reduces the compound to metal grains embedded in a lithium compound, for an oxide MxOy + 2yLi+ + 2ye− ⇌ yLi2O + xM, and on the way back the compound forms again. Oxides, sulfides, phosphides and fluorides work this way, with high capacities: about 1000 mAh/g for Fe2O3, 1230 for MnO2, 670 for MoS2 and 900 for FeP. Nano-sized transition-metal oxides were shown to store two to three times the capacity of carbon in 2000."],[12,"p","f9-6","figure 12.4","The drawbacks are four: the particles swell and pulverize; they conduct poorly (the Li2O formed is an insulator); the SEI is unstable; and the voltage hysteresis is about 1 V, because several solid phases must form and dissolve and oxygen and metal atoms must rearrange, not just lithium. On top of that, the first cycle loses about 30 %, and the wasted energy is the main obstacle. Nano-sized particles shorten the paths and spread the stress, but increase the surface that reacts with the electrolyte."],[12,"p","f9-6","figure 12.4","The same chemistry also appears on the other side of the cell. Transition-metal fluorides work the same way as positive electrodes, with theoretical capacities of 500 to 1500 mAh/g, and sulfur, at 2.4 V, is also a conversion electrode."],[12,"f","f9-7","Figure 12.5","Lithium metal: dendrites and dead lithium. Try this: play ten cycles at 1 mA/cm2, then reset the page and play at 4 mA/cm2; then switch to the ceramic separator."],[12,"s","s-lithium-metal","Lithium metal",""],[12,"p","s-lithium-metal","Lithium metal","We now reach the far corner of the map. Lithium metal is the most attractive negative electrode of all: 3860 mAh/g, the lowest potential, and nothing in it but active material. It was studied long before graphite, and kept out of commercial cells by dendrites. In 2004 lithium-metal cells reached about 100 to 150 cycles, against the 300 a commercial cell needed. Its problems are dendrites, an unstable SEI, low Coulombic efficiency, dead lithium, volume changes and pulverization, and reaction with the electrolyte."],[12,"p","s-lithium-metal","Lithium metal","Why does lithium plate back so unevenly? Several models explain the dendrites. In the space-charge model, when plating outruns ion conduction at high current, the Li+ concentration near the surface falls to zero and dendritic growth begins. Cracks in the SEI, tips and surface defects concentrate the electric field, and lithium grows fastest there. On stripping, pieces break off the dendrites and lose contact: dead lithium, which in one study thickened from about 2 µm to about 100 µm within a few dozen cycles, lowering the efficiency and raising the impedance. Whiskers that pierce the separator short the cell, with a risk of fire."],[12,"p","s-lithium-metal","Lithium metal","Most of these problems start at the interface, not in the bulk metal, which is why coatings, artificial SEI layers and new electrolytes are where the work is. The interface is studied with surface spectroscopy (XPS, soft X-ray absorption), optical and electron microscopy, cryo-electron microscopy, which preserves fragile interphase compounds, and synchrotron X-ray diffraction and pair-distribution analysis, which identified the much-debated LiH in the interphase on lithium metal."],[12,"p","s-lithium-metal","Lithium metal","Lithium metal stores the most, but it plates back unevenly, and that, not the chemistry of storage, is what limits it."],[12,"f","f9-8","Figure 12.6","How many cycles? Coulombic efficiency against lithium in reserve. Try this: find the efficiency at which the anode-free cell lasts 100 cycles; then the one at which it lasts 500."],[12,"p","f9-8","figure 12.6","If every cycle loses a little lithium, how much lithium should the cell carry in reserve? An anode-free cell carries none: it is assembled with no lithium on the negative side at all, only a copper current collector. On the first charge the lithium in the positive electrode is plated onto the copper, and on discharge it goes back. That is the highest energy a lithium-metal cell can reach, and the simplest to make; but with no lithium in reserve, every loss shows directly as lost capacity."],[12,"f","f9-9","Figure 12.7","Making anode-free cells work: three places to intervene. Try this: step through the four callouts and, for each fix, find the number that shows it worked."],[12,"p","f9-9","figure 12.7","The efficiency, then, is what we must raise, and three kinds of fix are being tried. An artificial SEI, made beforehand on the copper or formed in the cell, guides the lithium to deposit evenly. Electrolyte engineering: additives that build a better SEI, two-salt electrolytes, localized high-concentration electrolytes that mix a dissolving solvent with a diluent that does not dissolve the salt, and fluorinated solvents that are stable against both lithium and nickel-rich positive electrodes. And modified current collectors, such as three-dimensional porous copper that spreads the current and gives the lithium room."],[12,"p","f9-9","figure 12.7","Lithium metal was long thought too reactive to cycle in a liquid electrolyte. The new electrolytes have changed that: they now allow promising cycling with little excess lithium and heavily loaded positive electrodes."],[13,"p","","","Why are today's cells built on NMC or LFP, and what comes next?"],[13,"f","f9-12","Figure 13.1","Positive electrodes side by side, and what nickel buys. Try this: in Chemistries, tap NMC811 and then LFP; in the NMC view, compare NMC622 at 4.4 V with NMC811 at 4.2 V."],[13,"s","s-the-other-side-positive-electrodes","The other side: positive electrodes",""],[13,"p","s-the-other-side-positive-electrodes","The other side: positive electrodes","So far we have changed only the negative electrode. Let us now cross to the other side, because in today’s cells it is the positive electrode that holds the cell back: its capacity lags behind that of the negative electrode, and its materials account for around half the cost of a cell."],[13,"p","s-the-other-side-positive-electrodes","The other side: positive electrodes","Almost every commercial positive electrode belongs to one of three families of oxide, identified in Goodenough’s group in the 1980s: layered oxides such as LiCoO2, spinels such as LiMn2O4, and polyanion oxides such as LiFePO4. Oxides rather than sulfides, because the top of the oxide-ion band lies lower in energy, which lets a cell reach about 4 V instead of under 2.5 V."],[13,"p","s-the-other-side-positive-electrodes","The other side: positive electrodes","The three differ in how the lithium moves and how tightly the oxygen is held. In a layered oxide, lithium moves in planes between sheets of transition-metal oxide; these materials conduct well and pack densely. The spinel gives lithium three-dimensional paths, which favours power, but in practice it is limited to LiMn2O4, whose manganese dissolves and poisons the graphite. In a polyanion oxide, the strongly covalent P–O or S–O bonds weaken the covalency of the metal–oxygen bonds, lower the energy of the metal’s redox couple and so raise the voltage, the inductive effect: Fe2+/Fe3+ works below 2.5 V in Fe2O3 but at 3.6 V in Fe2(SO4)3. Oxygen held by phosphorus makes these materials thermally stable and safe, and iron makes them abundant; but they conduct poorly, so their particles must be small and coated with carbon, and they pack less densely."],[13,"s","s-from-licoo-to-nickel-rich-nmc","From LiCoO2 to nickel-rich NMC",""],[13,"p","s-from-licoo-to-nickel-rich-nmc","From LiCoO2 to nickel-rich NMC","LiCoO2 gives up only about half its lithium, because beyond that the Co3+/Co4+ band overlaps the top of the oxide band and oxygen leaves the lattice. Replacing cobalt with nickel and manganese gives NMC, LiNi1−y−zMnyCozO2, and each metal brings something. Nickel brings the capacity: it can be oxidized from Ni2+ all the way to Ni4+ without much loss of oxygen. Manganese, as Mn4+, stabilizes the structure without taking part in the redox. Cobalt keeps the layers ordered and the kinetics fast; in NCA, aluminium in place of manganese adds thermal stability."],[13,"p","s-from-licoo-to-nickel-rich-nmc","From LiCoO2 to nickel-rich NMC","More nickel means that more lithium can be taken out within the same voltage window, and NMC811 gives about 200 mAh/g. The price is stability. Ni4+ is unstable against the electrolyte; nickel and manganese dissolve, migrate to the graphite and thicken its SEI, and the phase changes at high charge crack the particles and open new surface. In one comparison of full cells against graphite, NMC111 and NMC622 cycled stably when charged to 4.4 V and NMC811 only to 4.0 V; above those limits the impedance of the positive electrode grew, from the point where each began to release oxygen from its surface, about 4.7 V vs Li for NMC111 and NMC622 and about 4.3 V for NMC811. The best stable result came from NMC622 charged to 4.4 V, about 650 mWh per gram of NMC (figure 13.1). Nickel-rich materials are also less thermally stable and more sensitive to air and moisture, which makes them harder and costlier to manufacture."],[13,"p","s-from-licoo-to-nickel-rich-nmc","From LiCoO2 to nickel-rich NMC","Which material wins depends on the use. Nickel-rich NMC and NCA serve long-range and high-performance cars; LFP, cheaper and longer-lived, has come back strongly, its uptake in electric vehicles doubling since 2020, and it serves grid storage and shorter-range cars. Cell and pack design have narrowed its energy gap: state-of-the-art LFP cells rose from 90 Wh/kg in 2010 to 160 Wh/kg in 2022, against about 300 Wh/kg for cells with NMC-type positive electrodes."],[13,"s","s-what-comes-next","What comes next",""],[13,"p","s-what-comes-next","What comes next","Several routes lead on. Single-crystal NMC particles avoid the cracks between primary grains that shorten the life of nickel-rich materials. Manganese-rich chemistries balance cost against range: LMFP, LiFePO4 with manganese added, reaches cells of 210 to 240 Wh/kg; LNMO works at about 4.7 V but waits for an electrolyte that survives there; lithium- and manganese-rich NMC offers about 280 mAh/g but loses voltage as it cycles. In 2023, none of these high-manganese cells had clearly passed technology readiness level 5. Conversion materials such as FeF3 and FeS2 promise 700 to 900 mAh/g at 1 to 2.5 V vs Li, at the price of large hysteresis, sluggish kinetics and strain. And molecules can take the transition metal out altogether, which is where we end this module."],[13,"p","s-what-comes-next","What comes next","A positive electrode is a compromise between capacity, voltage, stability and cost; more nickel buys capacity and spends margin."],[14,"p","","","Why replace the liquid with a solid, and why is it so hard?"],[14,"f","f7-8","Figure 14.1","Three ways to carry a lithium ion. Try this: cool the polymer and watch which of the three ions stops."],[14,"p","f7-8","figure 14.1","So far our ion has travelled through a liquid. Two other kinds of electrolyte carry lithium differently: a polymer, where there is no solvent at all, and a ceramic, where the ion moves through a solid framework. Let us take the polymer first."],[14,"p","f7-8","figure 14.1","A dry polymer such as poly(ethylene oxide), PEO, dissolves a lithium salt with no solvent at all. Wright and co-workers first saw ionic conduction in PEO complexes of sodium and potassium salts, and in 1978 Armand proposed the first dry PEO-lithium electrolyte for lithium batteries, conducting about 10−4 S/cm at 40 to 60 °C."],[14,"p","f7-8","figure 14.1","The Li+ ion is held by the oxygen atoms of the chain and hops from one coordinating site to the next, helped by the segmental motion of the chain; the motion happens in the amorphous, not the crystalline, regions of the polymer. That is the weakness of the idea: no chain motion, no conduction."],[14,"p","f7-8","figure 14.1","An electrolyte must carry ions and block electrons; how it carries them decides how fast, and at what temperature."],[14,"f","f7-9","Figure 14.2","Conductivity against temperature: the gaps follow the cited ratios, the heights are schematic. Try this: switch off everything except the two dry-polymer curves and read the gap between them at 40 °C."],[14,"p","f7-9","figure 14.2","How close can a polymer come to the liquid? The polymer route improved by steps. Changing the salt in PEO from lithium triflate, LiCF3SO3, to LiTFSI, a salt Armand introduced, gains about an order of magnitude, its bulky anion acting as a plasticizer; adding 10 to 25 % of a plasticizer gains another, and such lightly plasticized polymers still suit a lithium-metal electrode. Gels, 60 to 95 % liquid held in a polymer, come within 2 to 5 times of the liquid’s conductivity but need a lithium-ion configuration. A dry-polymer lithium cell runs at up to 80 °C."],[14,"p","f7-9","figure 14.2","LiTFSI itself is a good salt, conductive and safe, with one catch: above about 4 V (3.7 V in PC:DME, as we saw in figure 8.3) it corrodes the aluminium current collector unless a passivating salt is added. Liquid or polymer, a nonaqueous electrolyte is thermodynamically stable over a window of about 3.5 V but is used, kinetically, up to 5.5 V."],[14,"p","f7-9","figure 14.2","That window depends on more than the solvent alone, because the neighbours matter: a single EC molecule is computed to resist oxidation to about 6.9 V vs Li/Li+; next to a PF6− anion, which can take a hydrogen from it as HF, the limit falls, and when the hydrogen passes to a neighbouring EC it falls to about 5 to 5.2 V, almost 2 V lower. In the usual mixture, the cyclic EC dissolves the salt and builds the SEI, while a linear carbonate such as DMC thins the liquid so it conducts better in the cold."],[14,"f","f7-11","Figure 14.3","Why solid electrolytes are hard: four requirements, and a contact that has to survive breathing. Try this: press Play and watch for the moment the ion paths turn red."],[14,"p","f7-11","figure 14.3","That leaves the ceramic, and the question of why the liquid has not simply been replaced by a solid. A solid separator that blocks dendrites would make a lithium-metal negative electrode possible (chapter 12). To do so, a solid electrolyte must conduct Li+ at above 10−4 S/cm, block dendrites without being reduced by lithium, stay chemically stable, and form a robust, flexible thin membrane. The garnet Li7−ₓLa3Zr2−ₓTaₓO12 conducts above 10−3 S/cm and is stable against lithium; a 2013 assessment suggested that meeting all four requirements at once points to ceramic-polymer composites. A polymer above 10−4 S/cm with good electrode contact had, in 2013, yet to be demonstrated."],[14,"p","f7-11","figure 14.3","Even a good solid conductor has to keep touching the electrodes. Electrode particles swell and shrink as lithium goes in and out, and against an inorganic solid electrolyte the contact is lost. In a liquid, the electrolyte simply flows after them."],[14,"f","f9-10","Figure 14.4","Solid-state cells: the trouble is where the lithium meets the ceramic. Try this: at 1 mA/cm2, read the voltage lost at the bare ALD interface; then add the interlayer."],[14,"s","s-negative-electrodes-for-solid-state-cells","Negative electrodes for solid-state cells",""],[14,"p","s-negative-electrodes-for-solid-state-cells","Negative electrodes for solid-state cells","So far our lithium metal has faced a liquid. The alternative is to put a solid electrolyte against it, and the main reason for moving to solid electrolytes, which we met in chapter 14, is to use lithium metal. Graphite and titanate work in solid-state cells but store too little to be worth it; silicon is attractive but its swelling is hard on a rigid electrolyte. A solid electrolyte should be stiffer than the electrode to hold back dendrites, and lithium is soft; yet dendrites still grow through ceramic electrolytes, probably along their grain boundaries, and this is not yet understood."],[14,"p","s-negative-electrodes-for-solid-state-cells","Negative electrodes for solid-state cells","The lithium/ceramic interface has three problems: poor wetting, an unstable SEI and dendrites. Interlayers of Al2O3, LiNbO3 or ZrO2, often made by atomic layer deposition, a polymer-gel layer, or an additive such as dimethyl sulfate that forms a stiff Li2S/Li2O interphase, all improve it. Two other designs avoid the problem: columnar silicon, whose columns change volume anisotropically and so build up less stress, kept 92 % of its capacity after 50 cycles with a sulfide electrolyte against 50.6 % in a liquid one, at a Coulombic efficiency of about 99.95 %; and Samsung’s silver–carbon layer, which needs no excess lithium and gave a prototype pouch cell of more than 900 Wh/L that lasted 1000 cycles."],[15,"p","","","Can sodium, or molecules made of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen, do lithium's job?"],[15,"f","f9-11","Figure 15.1","Sodium is bigger. Try this: compare the gap the two ions are offered in the left-hand framework."],[15,"s","s-beyond-lithium","Beyond lithium",""],[15,"p","s-beyond-lithium","Beyond lithium","Every electrode in this module so far has stored lithium. Must it always be lithium? Lithium itself is a critical raw material, so its supply and sustainability matter too. A sodium-ion cell would rock sodium ions instead of lithium ions, on the principle of chapter 7. But Na+ is larger than Li+ and needs a larger interstitial space than many of the hosts that serve lithium offer, so sodium-ion cells need hosts of their own. The older sodium cells used molten electrodes instead: the sodium-sulfur battery, invented at Ford in 1967 after Kummer and Weber found fast Na+ conduction in a ceramic above 300 °C, runs at 300 to 350 °C. Sodium-ion is the first of the “beyond lithium” chemistries to become commercially viable, so far mainly for stationary storage, where energy density matters less."],[15,"f","f9-13","Figure 15.2","Organic electrodes: how they store charge, and how high their voltage now goes. Try this: in the ladder, tap Li4-p-DHT and then Mg(Li2)-p-DHT; the organic ion is the same, only the spectator cation has changed."],[15,"s","s-organic-electrodes","Organic electrodes",""],[15,"p","s-organic-electrodes","Organic electrodes","Every positive electrode so far stores its charge on a transition metal, which is mined and then processed at high temperature. Organic electrodes put the redox centre in a molecule or a polymer made of abundant light elements such as carbon, hydrogen, oxygen, nitrogen and sulfur; they can be made by environmentally friendly synthesis, and some could even be extracted from plants. Their potential can be tuned by molecular design, through the bonds or through space."],[15,"p","s-organic-electrodes","Organic electrodes","They are sorted by the charge the molecule takes on (figure 15.2). An n-type molecule is reduced, and a cation such as Li+ balances it; a p-type molecule is oxidized, and an anion from the electrolyte balances it; a bipolar one does both, in different windows of potential. The p-type materials work at relatively high potential but store the anion, so they suit dual-ion or lithium-free cells rather than the rocking chair; n-type materials work with the lithium ion itself."],[15,"p","s-organic-electrodes","Organic electrodes","The redox centre is a functional group: most often a conjugated carbonyl, which becomes an enolate when reduced, but also imines, organosulfur groups, nitroxide radicals and, recently, conjugated sulfonamides and oximates. Its potential follows the energy of the orbital that gives or takes the electron: groups that draw electron density away from the redox centre lower that orbital and raise the voltage. Most conventional n-type organic materials work below 3 V vs Li."],[15,"p","s-organic-electrodes","Organic electrodes","A lithium-ion cell adds a condition. Its graphite arrives empty, so the positive electrode must bring the lithium, as LiCoO2 does. An n-type organic made in its oxidized form brings none, and needs lithium metal as the negative electrode; the answer is to make it in its reduced, lithiated form. But a lithiated organic working below about 2.91 V vs Li is thermodynamically prone to oxidation by air, so most cannot even be handled in a dry room."],[15,"p","s-organic-electrodes","Organic electrodes","Small molecules also dissolve in the electrolyte, the main route of degradation of organic electrodes; making the material a polymer, building it into a framework, or replacing the liquid with a solid electrolyte are the usual remedies. And organic electrodes are usually mixed with a large share of conductive carbon, a quarter to 40 % of the electrode by mass in the studies cited here (a third in most of their half-cell tests)."],[15,"s","s-the-p-dht-family","The p-DHT family",""],[15,"p","s-the-p-dht-family","The p-DHT family","One family shows how far molecular design can go. Its parent is 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid, written p-DHT: the p, for para, marks that the two hydroxy groups sit opposite each other on the ring. The fully lithiated salt, dilithium (2,5-dilithium-oxy)-terephthalate or Li4-p-DHT, carries two kinds of redox group on one benzene ring: two phenolates (–OLi) that react at about 2.6 V and two carboxylates (–CO2Li) that react at about 0.8 V, so the same salt can serve as a positive or a negative electrode. The carboxylates also make it nearly insoluble in carbonate electrolytes. It can also be derived entirely from renewable resources."],[15,"p","s-the-p-dht-family","The p-DHT family","Its 2.6 V is too low for a modern cell, and here a subtle change works: two of the four lithium ions are replaced by one magnesium ion. The magnesium stays in place while lithium comes and goes, so it is called a spectator cation. The salt, magnesium (2,5-dilithium-oxy)-terephthalate or Mg(Li2)-p-DHT, has a name that reflects its synthesis: magnesium hydroxide first neutralizes the two carboxylic acid groups of 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid, and two equivalents of lithium hydroxide then neutralize the two phenol groups. The name might suggest that magnesium sits only at the carboxylates; the crystal structure, published in 2025, shows otherwise. Each Mg2+ sits in an octahedron of six oxygen atoms, four from phenolates and two from carboxylates. It is chelated by two p-DHT4− ions, each through a phenolate oxygen and the neighbouring carboxylate oxygen of that ion, closing two six-membered rings; two more phenolate oxygens complete the octahedron. Each phenolate oxygen, the redox centre, is bonded to one Li+ and two Mg2+ (see the structures below)."],[15,"p","s-the-p-dht-family","The p-DHT family","Mg2+ is small and doubly charged: it has a high ionic potential, its charge divided by its radius. Its bonds to oxygen are therefore more covalent. At the phenolate oxygen, bonded to two Mg2+, they draw electron density from that oxygen and through it from the ring; together with its bonds to the carboxylates, they weaken the electron donation (the donor inductive effect) of the –O− and –CO2− groups into the ring. The phenolate becomes harder to oxidize, and the potential rises by about 0.8 V, to about 3.4 V, in the range of LiFePO4; a calculation from the solved structure and the predicted half-delithiated one gives 3.45 V, in line with the measurement. Across the barium, lithium, calcium and magnesium salts, measured in the same way, the average potential rises almost linearly with the ionic potential of the spectator cation."],[15,"p","s-the-p-dht-family","The p-DHT family","Once dehydrated, the magnesium salt is stable in air, and it cycles stably near 100 mAh/g; but, like bulk Li4-p-DHT, it gives only one of its two possible electrons, whereas exfoliated nanosheets of Li4-p-DHT have been reported to reach the full two-electron capacity. The 2025 study explains why. After one electron is removed, each ring carries a radical; the rings, stacked face to face, pair up into dimers whose spins cancel, a Peierls distortion, and electron spin resonance finds only about 1.2 % of one unpaired spin per formula unit. Calculations indicate that removing the second electron would weaken or even break the C=O bonds of the carboxylates; charging bulk material beyond 4.0 V instead starts an irreversible reaction, attributed to Kolbe electrolysis."],[15,"p","s-the-p-dht-family","The p-DHT family","Changing the organic ion instead also works. In the ortho isomer, dilithium (2,3-dilithium-oxy)-terephthalate or Li4-o-DHT, the two –OLi groups sit side by side, and the potential rises by about 0.3 V; like the para salt, it is oxidized by air. Carboxylates held off the ring by a methylene (CH2) spacer, in tetralithium 2,5-dioxido-1,4-benzenediacetate (Li4-p-DOBDA), are no longer conjugated with it; the potential still rises, to 3.35 V, which the authors attribute to a through-space charge modulation in the solid, with both electrons exchanged, 215 mAh/g, though with a large polarization on cycling."],[15,"f","f9-14","Figure 15.3","The p-DHT family: the organic ions in 3D, and where the cations sit. Try this: turn p-DHT4− and then o-DHT4− and find the two ringed oxygens; then open the cations view and count the metal ions around one phenolate oxygen."],[15,"p","f9-14","figure 15.3","From my own work. My doctorate took Mg(Li2)-p-DHT as its benchmark positive electrode and built it into soft solid-state cells against lithium metal, with a solid polymer electrolyte that limits the dissolution of the organic material; it also developed a route to recover the organic precursor from the cycled electrode material."],[15,"s","s-beyond-carbonyl-chemistry-conjugated-sulfonamide","Beyond carbonyl chemistry: conjugated sulfonamides and oximates",""],[15,"p","s-beyond-carbonyl-chemistry-conjugated-sulfonamide","Beyond carbonyl chemistry: conjugated sulfonamides and oximates","Most lithiated organic positive electrodes, the p-DHT family included, rest on enolate and carbonyl chemistry. A newer line uses a different redox-active unit: a p-phenylenediamine core whose two nitrogen atoms each carry an electron-withdrawing group, methanesulfonyl (–SO2CH3) in the conjugated sulfonamides, trifluoromethanesulfonyl (–SO2CF3) in the conjugated triflimides and cyano (–C≡N) in the cyanamides. Conjugation spreads the negative charge of the lithiated form over the whole core and its withdrawing groups. On charge the core gives up two electrons in two steps, through a radical anion, to a neutral quinone diimine form: the Wurster-type redox of p-phenylenediamines. Because its charge is spread out, the anion is less nucleophilic, so the lithiated material resists water and oxygen in ambient air and still reacts reversibly at high potential."],[15,"p","s-beyond-carbonyl-chemistry-conjugated-sulfonamide","Beyond carbonyl chemistry: conjugated sulfonamides and oximates","Trifluoromethanesulfonyl withdraws electrons more strongly than methanesulfonyl, and the swap raised the potential by 0.5 V: the conjugated triflimides (the authors’ name for the family) work at 3.26 to 3.8 V, the highest reported for n-type organics by 2024, and are stable in moist ambient air. The simplest cyanamide, Li2-PDCA, stores more, 320 mAh/g in theory, but its fully charged form dissolves in the electrolyte. A third class, the conjugated oximates, carries its charge on C=N–O− groups; one of them, Li2-BQDO, reaches about 350 mAh/g at about 3.0 V, close to 1 kWh/kg at the material level."],[15,"p","s-beyond-carbonyl-chemistry-conjugated-sulfonamide","Beyond carbonyl chemistry: conjugated sulfonamides and oximates","Cycling stability remains a concern for most organic electrode materials, and it is too early to think of commercializing the newest of them. But their voltages now reach those of LiFePO4 and beyond, without cobalt or nickel."],[15,"p","s-beyond-carbonyl-chemistry-conjugated-sulfonamide","Beyond carbonyl chemistry: conjugated sulfonamides and oximates","In an organic electrode the molecule is the host; its voltage is set by the groups around the redox centre, and even by the cation beside it."],[15,"p","s-beyond-carbonyl-chemistry-conjugated-sulfonamide","Beyond carbonyl chemistry: conjugated sulfonamides and oximates","You can now explain where each family of negative electrode sits on the map of capacity and potential and why extra capacity has diminishing returns; the three ways to store lithium; how graphite’s SEI forms and how graphite ages hot and cold; why fast charging risks plating and how titanate and niobium oxides avoid it; why silicon and conversion materials are hard to cycle; what limits lithium metal and anode-free cells, and the fixes; why the lithium/ceramic interface matters in solid-state cells; why sodium needs hosts of its own; the three families of positive electrode and what nickel, manganese and cobalt each bring; why nickel-rich NMC trades stability for capacity; and how organic electrodes store charge and how their voltage is tuned."],[15,"p","s-beyond-carbonyl-chemistry-conjugated-sulfonamide","Beyond carbonyl chemistry: conjugated sulfonamides and oximates","We have taken both electrodes from today’s materials towards tomorrow’s. Yet even a well-behaved cell wears out. Why? That is the question of chapter 16."],[16,"p","","","Why does my battery hold less after a few years?"],[16,"p","","","Where we are. In chapter 15 we pushed both electrodes beyond today’s materials, towards lithium metal, nickel-rich oxides and organic molecules, and met materials that fail fast and loudly. A well-made lithium-ion cell fails slowly and quietly: a little capacity gone each week, whether it is used or not. In this module we ask where that capacity goes: we name the thieves and see how each one works."],[16,"f","f10-1","Figure 16.1","Capacity fade, taken apart into three thieves. Try this: switch off the lithium loss and see how much longer the cell lives; then press Measure at a low rate."],[16,"p","f10-1","figure 16.1","Let us start from the symptom: the cell holds less than it did. Capacity is lost in two ways. Some is only hidden: at a high current the cell reaches its cut-off early, and the capacity comes back at a gentler rate. The rest is gone for good, to changes in the volume of the electrodes, to reactions between electrodes and electrolyte, and to decomposition of the electrodes; the cycle life ends when the capacity has faded to 80 % of its initial value. In lithium-ion cells, poor lifetimes are rooted mainly in side reactions at the electrode-electrolyte interface."],[16,"p","f10-1","figure 16.1","Sorted by what they take, these causes are the three thieves of figure 10.1. Side reactions lock up lithium that could have carried charge. Cracking, contact loss and dissolution remove active material, the room the lithium needs. And thicker interfaces raise the resistance, so that at a given current the cell runs out of voltage before it runs out of charge."],[16,"p","f10-1","figure 16.1","A battery rarely dies of one cause; diagnosing it means separating the causes."],[16,"f","f5-10","Figure 16.2","Why 99.9 % per cycle is not good enough. Try this: find the lowest efficiency at which the blue line still crosses the 300-cycle line above 80 %."],[16,"p","f5-10","figure 16.2","Ageing leads to a practical question: how long does a cell last? Two numbers summarize a cell’s life. The Coulombic efficiency is the charge returned on discharge divided by the charge put in on charge, as a percentage; the cycle life is the number of cycles until the capacity has faded to 80 % of its initial value."],[16,"p","f5-10","figure 16.2","In 2004 a commercial rechargeable cell was expected to survive at least 300 full cycles with less than 20 % loss; most needed three to eight hours to charge while users wanted a quarter of an hour, and fast charging shortens cycle life. LiFePO4, with a much longer cycle life and calendar life than most NMC cells, is now used for grid storage and shorter-range electric vehicles. The fast-charging goal is 80 % of the charge in under 15 minutes."],[16,"p","f5-10","figure 16.2","Not every loss is permanent. Capacity hidden by a high rate comes back at a low one; what does not come back is lost to changes in the electrodes’ volume, to reactions between electrodes and electrolyte, and to decomposition of the electrodes. The first charge of a lithium-ion cell is a special case, a loss paid once (chapter 10)."],[16,"p","f5-10","figure 16.2","Lifetime is set by tiny losses repeated hundreds of times."],[16,"f","f10-2","Figure 16.3","The SEI keeps growing, and the exponent tells you why. Try this: both laws start from the same loss after the first month; compare where b = 1/2 and b = 1 end, then imagine the cell stored full and warm."],[16,"p","f10-2","figure 16.3","Let us follow the first thief, the one that takes lithium. The SEI of chapter 10 does not stop at the first charge. It keeps growing, more slowly, adding to the interfacial impedance as it evolves. Its thickness follows a power law in time, L ∝ tb, and so does the capacity lost to it: b = 1/2 when the supply through the layer is limited by diffusion, the usual assumption; b = 1 when the reaction itself, or migration during charging, is the bottleneck; b = 0 for migration during discharge. It grows fastest when the cell sits at a high state of charge and is charged fast. Too thick a layer means lost capacity and higher impedance at once."],[16,"p","f10-2","figure 16.3","How much does it matter? In storage, most of the capacity a lithium-ion cell loses goes into the SEI, and in a well-engineered cell more than half of all capacity loss can be traced to its growth. The layer grows while the graphite is being filled, not while it is being emptied, and in a charged cell it keeps growing at rest, more slowly; in the standard models only a fully discharged cell stops feeding it. On silicon the picture changes: the large volume change keeps cracking the SEI, and the growth becomes linear in time."],[16,"f","f10-3","Figure 16.4","Manganese on the move: one electrode’s loss becomes the other’s problem. Try this: count how many manganese ions it takes before the SEI is marked as working worse; the threshold is a drawing choice, not a value from the cited work."],[16,"p","f10-3","figure 16.4","So far we have watched one thief at a time. They can also work together. In a cell with a spinel LiMn2O4 positive electrode, manganese dissolves as Mn(II), crosses the electrolyte and poisons the SEI on the negative electrode. The positive electrode loses active material; the negative electrode’s protection weakens, and with it more lithium is lost. Particles that crack and lose contact, as alloys do (figure 7.3), are the other route to losing active material."],[16,"f","f10-4","Figure 16.5","On the shelf: self-discharge differs by orders of magnitude between chemistries. Try this: count the decades on the axis between the two dots."],[16,"p","f10-4","figure 16.5","Cells also age when nothing is connected, on the shelf. Self-discharge, the slow loss of charge with nothing connected, differs enormously between chemistries: a nickel-metal hydride cell can lose up to 30 % a month, while a lithium-MnO2 primary cell keeps 90 % after eight years. It rises with temperature. Overcharging or overdischarging a cell is another way to age it fast: both drive irreversible reactions that form new compounds and cost capacity."],[16,"p","f10-4","figure 16.5","You can now explain the difference between capacity that is hidden and capacity that is lost, the three thieves and how they cooperate, why the SEI keeps costing lithium and how fast, and why a cell ages on the shelf as well as in use."],[16,"p","f10-4","figure 16.5","To catch a thief we need a witness. Next we see how these losses are measured, and how the measurements tell one thief from another."],[16,"p","f10-4","figure 16.5","The square-root law is the usual reading of storage data, and it is what a growth limited by something diffusing through the layer predicts. Which something is still debated: solvent through the pores, electrons through the compact inner layer, or neutral lithium atoms; storage tests at different states of charge favour the last. A recent comparison adds two cautions: few of the √t fits in the literature are statistically justified, and none of the standard models reproduces how much larger the loss in the first cycle is than in the later ones. The shape of a fade curve alone cannot prove a mechanism."],[16,"p","f10-4","figure 16.5","The lithium a new cell locks into its SEI on the first charge is gone before the cell is ever used, and in conversion electrodes the first-cycle loss reaches about 30 %. Can we pay it back? One remedy, proposed by Armand, is sacrificial salts: lithium salts with an oxidizable anion mixed into the positive electrode. On the first charge the anion is oxidized and gives up its lithium, which pays for the SEI; what is left of the salt leaves pores behind that help the electrolyte wet the electrode. The salt is chosen by its oxidation potential to suit the cell, and the idea extends to sodium and potassium cells."],[17,"p","","","Can a battery catch fire, and how do we keep it safe?"],[17,"p","","","Where we are. In Lab 1 we learned to measure a cell and to find out what has gone wrong inside it. Two questions remain, and they matter most to the person holding the cell. What happens when it is abused, so that its stored energy comes out all at once? And what becomes of its materials when it is finished?"],[17,"f","f12-1","Figure 17.1","The runaway loop: heat that makes more heat. Try this: slide the cooling down slowly and find where the trace stops levelling off."],[17,"p","f12-1","figure 17.1","Let us begin with the energy itself. A charged cell holds its energy in two materials kept apart. If they meet through a short circuit, the whole stored energy comes out as heat. The worst case is thermal runaway: the reaction of an electrode with the electrolyte becomes self-sustaining and enters an autocatalytic mode, the heat it releases speeding it up. Because this heat is released inside the cell, at the electrode surfaces, cells meant for high currents must be built to shed it. The SEI we met in chapter 10 plays a part here too: its decomposition promotes thermal runaway."],[17,"p","f12-1","figure 17.1","The history of the rechargeable lithium cell is written in fires. Exxon’s rechargeable lithium-metal cell of the 1970s grew dendrites across the electrolyte to the positive electrode; the internal short ignited the flammable electrolyte and the programme was abandoned. In 1989 the first lithium-metal rechargeable cell sold for consumer use, Moli Energy’s Li/MoS2, was recalled after cells vented with flame in the field: the porous, high-surface lithium deposited by cycling reacts with the electrolyte strongly enough to do that, and cycling makes cells less tolerant of abuse."],[17,"p","f12-1","figure 17.1","A cell is safe as long as its heat can leave faster than its reactions make it."],[17,"f","f12-2","Figure 17.2","Layers of protection, and which one answers which kind of abuse. Try this: choose Short inside and see why prevention matters more than any switch."],[17,"s","s-electronics-and-separators","Electronics and separators",""],[17,"p","s-electronics-and-separators","Electronics and separators","If a cell is safe only while its heat leaves faster than its reactions make it, the first task is to stop the faults that start those reactions. Let us go through the safeguards from the outside in. Lithium cells have no built-in chemical way of shedding overcharge, unlike nickel-cadmium, and in a battery of cells in series small differences in capacity and self-discharge push individual cells out of balance. So the end of charge and of discharge is controlled by watching the voltage, cell by cell; Sony’s first lithium-ion batteries carried a circuit that did exactly that and also limited the current."],[17,"p","s-electronics-and-separators","Electronics and separators","Inside the cell, the next safeguard is the separator. A separator that loses its porosity when it gets hot stops the ions in a fault that heats the cell evenly, such as a short outside it, but helps much less against a local internal short. A ceramic Al2O3 coating on the separator helps keep dendrites from getting through."],[17,"s","s-the-electrolyte-as-a-safeguard","The electrolyte as a safeguard",""],[17,"p","s-the-electrolyte-as-a-safeguard","The electrolyte as a safeguard","The electrolyte can be a safeguard too. To see how, consider what overcharge does to the cell. An overcharged cell loses too much lithium from its positive electrode, whose crystal starts to release oxygen and becomes unstable, while lithium metal deposits on the negative electrode; the electrodes then decompose the solvents, and a sudden exothermic reaction follows. Additives in small quantities answer this in three main ways: redox shuttles consume the excess current through a redox process and keep the voltage from rising; aromatic compounds such as biphenyl are believed to polymerize above the maximum operating voltage into an insulating layer on the positive electrode, raising the cell’s resistance; and compounds such as cyclohexylbenzene decompose and give off hydrogen, so that a pressure-operated current interrupter cuts the charging current. None is free: additives always have side effects, and with biphenyl, cycle life at 40 °C up to 4.3 V got worse the more of it there was."],[17,"p","s-the-electrolyte-as-a-safeguard","The electrolyte as a safeguard","The electrolyte itself could also be made safer. A nonflammable liquid is a further aim: phosphate esters such as triethyl phosphate have very high flash points, but trimethyl phosphate is unstable against graphite, which led some to judge such electrolytes impractical."],[17,"s","s-safety-built-into-the-chemistry","Safety built into the chemistry",""],[17,"p","s-safety-built-into-the-chemistry","Safety built into the chemistry","The innermost safeguard is the chemistry itself. The lithium-metal polymer cells that Bolloré has used in electric cars and buses since 2011 are an example: solid polymer electrolytes, more than 3000 cycles without dendrites."],[17,"f","f12-3","Figure 17.3","The comfort zone. Try this: find the narrowest band, and think where your phone spends its summer."],[17,"p","f12-3","figure 17.3","So far we have looked at abuse. Ordinary use matters too: temperature and state of charge decide how a cell ages, and we take temperature first. Consumer cells are designed to work between about 0 and 40 °C and to be stored between −20 and 85 °C; military and automotive cells must work from −50 to 85 °C. Within those limits, three trends matter. Self-discharge rises with temperature. The SEI grows fastest when the cell sits at a high state of charge and is charged fast. And at high current and low temperature, lithium can plate on the graphite instead of entering it."],[17,"p","f12-3","figure 17.3","What that means in your pocket. Read together, those statements favour keeping a cell cool, not leaving it full for long periods, and not fast-charging it in the cold. This is our own reading of the three statements above, not a rule stated in any of the works cited."],[18,"p","","","What happens to a battery when it is finished?"],[18,"f","f12-4","Figure 18.1","The recycling routes: three ways back into a new cell. Try this: follow the blue dot and, at each route, note what is recovered and what is lost."],[18,"p","f12-4","figure 18.1","Even a cell that is used kindly reaches the end of its life. What becomes of it then? Recycling a lithium-ion battery means recovering above all its cobalt, lithium and nickel, besides aluminium and copper. Two chemical routes dominate: pyrometallurgy, which smelts the cells at about 1000 °C into a copper-nickel-cobalt alloy while lithium and manganese go to the slag, and hydrometallurgy, which dissolves the electrode materials in acid and separates the metals, with higher recovery and lower energy use but more complex processing. Direct recycling, which keeps the electrode materials whole, is still immature."],[18,"p","f12-4","figure 18.1","Why does this matter? Cobalt is scarce, costly and toxic, which is one reason the positive electrode has moved from LiCoO2 toward materials with less of it. Lead-acid batteries show what a closed loop can look like: by a 2004 account, about 98 % of them were recycled in the United States."],[18,"p","f12-4","figure 18.1","A loop can only close if the spent cells are collected in the first place. For lithium batteries, a 2020 European proposal would raise the collection target for portable batteries from 45 % to 65 % by 2025 and 70 % by 2030, and require all batteries from electric vehicles and stationary storage to be collected and recycled."],[18,"p","f12-4","figure 18.1","From my own work. The organic electrode materials of my doctorate were designed with their end of life in mind: I developed a route that recovers the organic precursor from a cycled electrode material, Mg(Li2)-p-DHT, and separates the streams containing lithium and magnesium (manuscript in preparation)."],[18,"p","f12-4","figure 18.1","You can now explain what thermal runaway is and what breaks the loop, why lithium cells need electronics as well as chemistry to stay safe, why temperature and state of charge decide how a cell ages, and the three ways its materials can go back into a new cell."],[18,"p","f12-4","figure 18.1","This is where my own work starts: the research behind these pages."],[19,"p","","","How is a test cell made, and what does a cycling test tell us?"],[19,"p","","","Where we are. In chapter 16 we named the thieves that take a cell’s capacity. From the outside they all look alike: the cell holds less. How can we see these losses from outside, and tell one from another? In this module we build up the measurements that do it, from a single current pulse to a full impedance spectrum, and we learn to read the results honestly: what each one answers, when its formula holds, and how to analyse the data without fooling ourselves."],[19,"f","f11-1","Figure L1.1","One cell, four measurements, four questions. Try this: for each panel, name the thief of chapter 16 it would help to catch."],[19,"p","f11-1","figure L1.1","Let us put your cell back on the test bench of chapter 5. Every measurement answers one question, so we choose the question first. Cycling at constant current gives the capacity, the effect of rate and temperature, and the state of health; switching the current off and timing the recovery separates the three polarizations (figure 5.4). A short current pulse gives a resistance. The galvanostatic intermittent titration technique, GITT, measures how fast lithium diffuses inside the electrode material. Impedance spectroscopy separates the processes in a cell by their speed."],[19,"p","f11-1","figure L1.1","We take them in that order, from the simplest reading to the analysis behind published diffusion coefficients and impedance spectra. On the way we lean on three tools from chapter 5: the test bench (figure 5.1), the three clocks of polarization (figure 5.4) and differential capacity (figure L1.2)."],[19,"p","f11-1","figure L1.1","Every technique answers one question; choosing the question comes first."],[19,"f","f5-9","Figure L1.2","Reading a curve by its slopes, and what ageing does to them. Try this: set LLI to 0, then raise it slowly and watch which dV/dQ peaks move and which stay put."],[19,"p","f5-9","figure L1.2","We now know enough to read a real curve. A voltage curve hides a lot in its gentle bends. Plot its slope instead and the bends become peaks. Two plots are used: the differential voltage dV/dQ against charge, and the differential capacity dQ/dV against voltage."],[19,"p","f5-9","figure L1.2","Peaks in differential capacity correspond to plateaus, where two phases are in equilibrium. Materials that take lithium as one smooth solid solution show no sharp peaks. Put compactly: “A peak in the [dQ/dV] plot features the equilibrium between different electrochemically-active phases in the electrode whereas a peak in the [dV/dQ] plot locates the transition from one equilibrium to another.”"],[19,"s","s-two-electrodes-in-one-curve","Two electrodes in one curve",""],[19,"p","s-two-electrodes-in-one-curve","Two electrodes in one curve","Where do the peaks come from? A cell’s voltage is the positive electrode’s potential minus the negative’s, each set by how much lithium that electrode holds:"],[19,"p","s-two-electrodes-in-one-curve","Two electrodes in one curve","The two electrodes’ slopes simply add, so every dV/dQ peak of the cell can be traced to one electrode. A dQ/dV peak needs the voltage to stand still while charge flows, so both electrodes must be on a plateau at once."],[19,"s","s-the-square-plot-and-ageing","The square plot and ageing",""],[19,"p","s-the-square-plot-and-ageing","The square plot and ageing","To see both electrodes at once, we draw the state of a cell on a square plot: the negative electrode’s lithium content across, the positive electrode’s (inverted) up. A cell charging from empty to full is a straight line on it. The slope of the line is the ratio of the electrodes’ capacities, and where it meets the left edge measures the lithium the cell has lost for good."],[19,"p","s-the-square-plot-and-ageing","The square plot and ageing","Now let the cell age. Ageing comes in two classic kinds. Loss of lithium inventory (LLI) is cyclable lithium used up by side reactions. Loss of active material (LAM) is material that has changed irreversibly, dissolved or come loose from the current collector. LLI moves the line up; LAM tilts it, losing positive material steepens it and losing negative material flattens it. The window between the cut-offs then picks out a shorter piece of line, so the capacity falls."],[19,"p","s-the-square-plot-and-ageing","The square plot and ageing","In one LiCoO2 and graphite example, lost lithium leaves the graphite peaks where they were on the charge axis but moves the LiCoO2 peaks to lower charge. The toy cell does the same. Differentiating real data is sensitive to noise: in that analysis, slow C/20 data were thinned 144-fold before differentiating, to reduce the noise."],[19,"p","s-the-square-plot-and-ageing","The square plot and ageing","Slopes turn a smooth curve into peaks that can be traced to each electrode, and their shifts tell lost lithium from lost material."],[20,"p","","","How do we measure resistance and diffusion?"],[20,"f","f11-2","Figure L2.1","The resistance of a cell depends on when you read it. Try this: read the resistance at 10 ms and at 10 s; one is more than half as large again as the other."],[20,"s","s-the-simplest-reading-a-current-pulse","The simplest reading: a current pulse",""],[20,"p","s-the-simplest-reading-a-current-pulse","The simplest reading: a current pulse","We begin with the simplest question: how much does the cell resist? Let us switch on a constant current and watch the voltage. It jumps at once; the jump divided by the current, ΔV/ΔI , is the ohmic resistance of electrolyte, active material, current collectors and contacts. Industry calls this the DC internal resistance. After the jump the voltage keeps moving, first as the interfaces charge (charge transfer), then, more slowly, as diffusion sets in."],[20,"p","s-the-simplest-reading-a-current-pulse","The simplest reading: a current pulse","So the value we get depends on when we read it, and also on how fast the instrument samples, on the size of the current, on heating and on the change of state of charge during the pulse. That is why test standards specify pulse lengths and currents for “pulse power” tests. A pulse of length t probes roughly what an alternating current of frequency 1/(2πt) probes: values from the two methods approximately agree when the timescales match."],[20,"p","s-the-simplest-reading-a-current-pulse","The simplest reading: a current pulse","A resistance quoted without its timescale is half a number."],[20,"f","f11-3","Figure L2.2","GITT: one pulse, one rest, two voltage changes. Try this: pause just before the current switches off and look at the profile: the surface is far ahead of the back of the film."],[20,"s","s-gitt-a-diffusion-coefficient-from-a-voltage","GITT: a diffusion coefficient from a voltage",""],[20,"p","s-gitt-a-diffusion-coefficient-from-a-voltage","GITT: a diffusion coefficient from a voltage","A pulse tells us how much the cell resists, but the slow, diffusive part of its answer is mixed in with everything else. If we keep the pulse short, let the cell rest afterwards and repeat, the voltage curve becomes a diffusion measurement: this is GITT. It starts from equilibrium, with the same lithium content everywhere. A constant current pulse of length τ removes (or adds) a known amount of lithium through the surface; a rest follows until the electrode is in equilibrium again; and the pair is repeated across the whole charge."],[20,"p","s-gitt-a-diffusion-coefficient-from-a-voltage","GITT: a diffusion coefficient from a voltage","During the pulse, lithium is taken from the surface faster than diffusion can refill it from inside, so the surface composition runs ahead and the voltage climbs: the transient change ΔEt=E3−E2 , measured after the immediate IR jump. After the rest the electrode has settled at a slightly different composition: the steady-state change ΔEs=E4−E1 , a step along the equilibrium curve. The slower the diffusion, the larger ΔEt compared with ΔEs . Weppner and Huggins turned the ratio into the chemical diffusion coefficient:"],[20,"p","s-gitt-a-diffusion-coefficient-from-a-voltage","GITT: a diffusion coefficient from a voltage","Here mB , MB and VM are the mass, molar mass and molar volume of the electrode material and S its area in contact with the electrolyte; the bracket mBVM/(MBS) is volume over area, the thickness of a film. For spherical particles of radius r it becomes r/3 . The IR jump is left out of ΔEt because it is not diffusion: it holds the ohmic and the charge-transfer drops."],[20,"f","f11-4","Figure L2.3","When is a GITT pulse short enough? Try this: switch to the sphere and lengthen the pulse until the formula is 20 % low; read Dτ/L2 there."],[20,"p","f11-4","figure L2.3","Before we trust such a number, we should ask what the formula assumes. It rests on two straight lines. First, the steady-state step must be small, so that the equilibrium curve is straight over it. Second, during the pulse the voltage must grow as t , which is true only while the disturbed layer near the surface is thin compared with the sample: the short-time solution of the diffusion equation."],[20,"p","f11-4","figure L2.3","What decides “short” is the pulse length in units of the time diffusion needs to cross the sample, τ^=Dτ/L2 . The recommended limit is τ^<0.25 for flat samples and about ten times less for spheres and cylinders, and one survey found that most published GITT studies used longer pulses. Since D is what we are measuring, we make the check afterwards, with the value just obtained. For spheres a second criterion applies: the t law is within 5 % of the exact solution only for Dt/r2<0.0032 ."],[20,"p","f11-4","figure L2.3","Particle size is the other trap. The formula needs the diffusion length, and a porous electrode has secondary particles several micrometres across made of primary grains a few hundred nanometres across. Because D scales with r2 , choosing 5 µm instead of 0.25 µm gives a value 400 times larger. A published diffusion coefficient means little without the radius it was referred to."],[20,"p","f11-4","figure L2.3","Short pulse, small step, known size: without all three, the GITT formula returns a number, not a diffusion coefficient."],[20,"f","f11-5","Figure L2.4","Six recipes, one pulse, different answers. Try this: slide τdl from 1 s to 100 s, as when the cell is cooled, and watch which bars leave the line at 1."],[20,"p","f11-5","figure L2.4","Even with a short enough pulse, the answer depends on how we read the voltage. In one study the same GITT data on an NCM523 positive electrode were analysed by six procedures: two formulas that need the IR drop read off the curve (P1, P2), a straight-line fit of E against t (P3), a two-line fit that leaves out the start (P4), and fits of the whole pulse to the spherical solution, with and without the start (P5, P6). At 40 °C the results differed by a factor of 3; on cooling, by more than an order of magnitude."],[20,"p","f11-5","figure L2.4","The reason is the start of the pulse. The ohmic drop is instant, but the charge-transfer drop arrives only as the double layer charges, with time constant τdl=RctCdl : below 1 s above 0 °C at medium lithium content, far longer in the cold. A recipe that reads the IR drop at a fixed 5 s, or fits a line through the start, mistakes double-layer charging for diffusion. Procedures that skip the transition (P4, P6) are expected to be more reliable; but if Rct itself changes during the pulse, the full fit P6 is thrown off most."],[20,"p","f11-5","figure L2.4","In practice this means: measure τdl by impedance before each pulse; use a three-electrode cell, so that the lithium counter electrode’s own overvoltage, large at low temperature, is not mistaken for slow diffusion; keep the current small (0.1C agreed with 0.05C); and check that two currents give the same answer."],[20,"f","f11-6","Figure L2.5","Read the rest, not the pulse. Try this: tick “resistance falls 17 %” and compare the two estimates; then lengthen the pulse and watch the straight part of the rest plot shrink."],[20,"p","f11-6","figure L2.5","The pulse itself is the weak point, so a more robust route reads diffusion from the rest after the pulse. During the pulse the voltage carries the IR drop on top of the diffusion signal, and if the total resistance drifts, as charge-transfer resistance does when the composition changes, the t slope is wrong. In one worked example, a 17 % fall in resistance during the pulse halves the slope and makes D four times too large. During the rest no current flows, so no IR drop can drift."],[20,"p","f11-6","figure L2.5","The rest can be solved exactly: plotted against t+τ−t , where t is the time since the current stopped, the voltage is a straight line whose slope gives D , without needing the final equilibrium voltage. Fast processes that are not bulk diffusion, such as a surface layer, appear at the start of the rest and can be left out of the fit; the finite size of the sample bends the curve near the end, so the straight part in between is used, which for a short pulse lasts up to about ten pulse lengths of rest. The recommended procedure: large, dense, single-phase samples; at least three pulses of different size; and the τ^ check afterwards."],[20,"p","f11-6","figure L2.5","The rest must also be long enough. In one study an NMC622 electrode needed about 7 to 9 h to relax below 3.8 V, and above 3.8 V the voltage was still drifting by 0.1 to 0.6 mV per hour after 9 to 12 h; published studies use fixed rests from 40 min to a day. The same study showed that ignoring diffusion in the electrolyte inside the pores, as the simple formula does, made its GITT value about four times smaller than a transmission-line analysis of impedance (figure L3.4)."],[20,"p","f11-6","figure L2.5","GITT is easy to run and easy to misread: check the pulse length, the reading of the IR drop, the rest and the size."],[20,"f","f11-7","Figure L2.6","A whole GITT run: three results from one experiment. Try this: open the Diffusion panel and untick “4 h rests”; find where the 1 h rest was not enough."],[20,"p","f11-7","figure L2.6","So far we have looked at one step at a time. A whole GITT run gives more than a diffusion coefficient. The voltage at the end of each rest is a quasi-open-circuit voltage (QOCV); joined up, these trace the equilibrium curve of the electrode. The voltage at the end of each pulse is the closed-circuit voltage (CCV). Their difference is the overpotential at that state of charge, and dividing by the current gives an internal resistance. With D from each step, the run yields three curves against composition."],[20,"p","f11-7","figure L2.6","The price is time: sixteen steps of 10 min with 4 h rests take nearly three days, and where relaxation is slow even 4 h may not be enough."],[21,"p","","","How do we record an impedance spectrum we can trust?"],[21,"f","f11-8","Figure L3.1","Impedance: a sine in, a sine out, compared in size and timing. Try this: choose R ‖ C and slide the frequency from 1 Hz to 10 kHz; watch the line open into an ellipse and close again."],[21,"s","s-impedance-spectroscopy-from-the-beginning","Impedance spectroscopy, from the beginning",""],[21,"p","s-impedance-spectroscopy-from-the-beginning","Impedance spectroscopy, from the beginning","Pulses and GITT work in time: we push the cell and watch every process answer on its own clock, all mixed in one voltage curve. Impedance spectroscopy works in frequency instead. It disturbs the cell with a small sine, a voltage or a current, and records the sine that answers, at many frequencies one after the other. If the cell behaves linearly and does not change during the measurement, the answer has the same frequency and differs only in size and timing. The ratio of the two amplitudes is |Z| ; the timing difference is the phase φ . Both fit in one complex number:"],[21,"p","s-impedance-spectroscopy-from-the-beginning","Impedance spectroscopy, from the beginning","Each circuit element has its own signature. A resistor: Z=R , in phase, at every frequency. A capacitor: Z=1/(jωC) , with the current leading by 90° and the size falling as frequency rises ( ω=2πf ). An inductor: Z=jωL , current lagging, size rising. Because the three depend so differently on frequency, sweeping the frequency separates them."],[21,"p","s-impedance-spectroscopy-from-the-beginning","Impedance spectroscopy, from the beginning","The instrument is a potentiostat with a frequency response analyser, which correlates the response with a sine and a cosine at the excitation frequency over whole periods to get the two parts of Z . Commercial instruments span about 10 µHz to 1 MHz, but slow points are expensive: a single measurement at 10 µHz takes 27.8 h, so in practice the lowest frequency is about 1 mHz, often 10 to 100 mHz. Sixty frequencies from 100 kHz down to 0.1 Hz take 2 to 3 minutes."],[21,"f","f11-9","Figure L3.2","One spectrum, two pictures. Try this: lower τ1/τ2 from 1000 to 1 and find where you could no longer tell that there are two processes."],[21,"p","f11-9","figure L3.2","Once we have Z at many frequencies, we need to draw it, and there are two standard ways. The Nyquist plot puts −Z″ upward against Z′ , one point per frequency, high frequencies on the left; a resistor in parallel with a capacitor draws a semicircle of diameter R . Its weaknesses: the frequency of each point is not shown, and the large low-frequency values set the scale and squash the high-frequency end. The Bode plot shows |Z| and the phase against the logarithm of frequency, so every point’s frequency can be read and each decade gets equal room."],[21,"p","f11-9","figure L3.2","Each R‖C pair has a time constant τ=RC , and its arc peaks where ωτ=1 , that is at f=1/(2πτ) . Two pairs give two separate arcs only if their time constants differ by about a factor of 100 or more; closer, the arcs merge, and at equal τ a single semicircle remains. One rule is often broken: the two axes of a Nyquist plot must have the same scale; stretching the vertical one makes depressed arcs look round."],[21,"f","f11-10","Figure L3.3","A commercial cell’s spectrum, read from left to right. Try this: step through the five callouts and name which part of the cell each new piece of the curve comes from."],[21,"p","f11-10","figure L3.3","With these two pictures we can read the spectrum of a real cell, from left to right, high frequency to low. At the top of the range the cables and the wound electrodes act as an inductance, so the spectrum starts below the axis. Where it crosses the axis is the ohmic resistance, the sum of electrolyte, active material, current collectors and contacts. In the middle range come arcs from the interfaces, each a resistance in parallel with a capacitance; at low frequency a tail from lithium diffusing inside the particles."],[21,"p","f11-10","figure L3.3","A commercial cell has large electrodes and so an impedance in the milliohm range, mostly below 0.1 Ω and at frequencies below a few tens of kilohertz. That makes it hard to measure: a small voltage drives a large current, of the order of an ampere. And the interpretation is ambiguous: each electrode has its own SEI and its own charge transfer, at least four processes, but with only two terminals arcs of similar time constant merge into one or two. Changing the state of charge or the temperature, which moves the processes by different amounts, helps tell them apart."],[21,"p","f11-10","figure L3.3","Manufacturers use a single frequency, typically 1 kHz, to check and match cells on the production line; researchers use full spectra to follow degradation, which is the most common reason impedance is measured on commercial cells."],[21,"p","f11-10","figure L3.3","Left to right is fast to slow: wires, ohmic parts, interfaces, diffusion."],[21,"f","f11-11","Figure L3.4","The shapes real cells draw, and the physics behind each. Try this: set n = 0.5 in panel A and compare with the 45° line in panel B; then slide the pore resistance to zero in panel D."],[21,"p","f11-11","figure L3.4","The spectrum of figure L3.3 is idealized; real spectra are never made of perfect semicircles. Arcs are depressed: their centres sit below the axis. The usual fix replaces the capacitor with a constant phase element, Z=1/(Y0(jω)n) : n=1 is a capacitor, n=0 a resistor and n=0.5 a Warburg diffusion line. Surface roughness, fractal geometry and adsorption have all been proposed as its cause, and the question is not settled. A CPE with a free exponent can fit almost any valid data, so its parameters cannot be read as real capacitances."],[21,"p","f11-11","figure L3.4","Diffusion into a layer of finite thickness starts as the 45° Warburg line, then bends. If the far side lets lithium through, the line bends back to the axis in an arc; if it blocks lithium, as the inside of a particle does, the line turns vertical like a capacitor. Particles are closer to spheres than to films: with the same diffusion resistance Rd and capacitance Cd , a sphere turns upward earlier and its tail stands at Rd/15 instead of Rd/3 , and the diffusion coefficient follows as D=r2/(RdCd) ."],[21,"p","f11-11","figure L3.4","A porous electrode is a ladder: the electrolyte resistance runs down each pore, and every grain wall has its own interface. The result is a 45° line at high frequency followed by an arc. In the same study, fitted to an NMC622 electrode, such a transmission-line model gave a diffusion coefficient about four times larger than GITT or impedance models that put diffusion in simple series."],[21,"f","f11-12","Figure L3.5","How small is small enough? Try this: find the amplitude where the distortion passes 1 %, then set α = 0.4 and watch the second harmonic appear."],[21,"s","s-measuring-well","Measuring well",""],[21,"p","s-measuring-well","Measuring well","So far we have trusted the measurement itself. Let us now check it, starting with the size of the sine. Impedance theory assumes a linear system, and an electrode reaction is not one: its current grows exponentially with overpotential. Close to equilibrium the Butler–Volmer curve is nearly straight with slope 1/Rct , and that straight piece is what impedance measures. A nonlinear response to a pure sine is distorted; it contains signals at 2f , 3f and higher, because the harmonics reflect the curvature of the curve."],[21,"p","s-measuring-well","Measuring well","The amplitude is therefore a compromise: large enough for a clean signal, small enough to stay linear. Under voltage control 5 to 10 mV is usual, and the Lissajous plot shows when it is too large. Under current control, 0.1 to 1 A on a commercial cell gives a response of a few millivolts and may be more accurate. The total harmonic distortion, the harmonics’ combined size over the fundamental, checks linearity at every frequency."],[21,"p","s-measuring-well","Measuring well","Nonlinear methods turn this round. NLEIS drives the cell moderately harder and reads the harmonics as “higher-order impedance”; NFRA uses large currents to excite them on purpose. In one study the ratio of third to second harmonic rose in cells aged at −10 °C, where lithium had plated, while ordinary spectra looked the same. There is no standard method yet, and large currents can damage the cell."],[21,"f","f11-13","Figure L3.6","For a cell of milliohms, how it is connected changes the answer. Try this: lengthen the cable to 5 m and read how far the two-terminal intercept has moved."],[21,"p","f11-13","figure L3.6","Next come the wires. With two terminals the analyser passes the current and measures the voltage through the same wires, so it measures the cell plus the wires. With four, one pair carries the current and a second pair senses the voltage at the cell’s own terminals; since the sense pair carries almost no current, its wires drop no voltage, and only the cell is measured. For batteries, where the wires are not negligible, four terminals are recommended."],[21,"p","f11-13","figure L3.6","Cables also bring inductance, proportional to their length and most visible on low-impedance cells: the high-frequency end of the spectrum drops further below the axis. Short cables, current and sense wires twisted in pairs, a metal box around the cell, the same cables for calibration and measurement, and a fixed holder all help; simply re-inserting a cell in its holder changed the ohmic resistance by up to 10 % in one study."],[21,"p","f11-13","figure L3.6","The cell must also be steady, and that takes several steps: set the state of charge, then rest until the voltage stops moving (4 h was found adequate in one study; others report 15 min to 100 h, and half of the surveyed studies did not say); let the temperature settle, remembering that the inside of the cell lags the chamber; and set the state of charge before changing the temperature."],[21,"f","f11-14","Figure L3.7","Is the spectrum valid? Test it before you model it. Try this: tick “cell drifting” with no noise, then add noise without drift, and compare the two residual patterns."],[21,"p","f11-14","figure L3.7","With a small sine, a good connection and a steady cell, one test remains before we model anything. A spectrum is worth modelling only if it could have come from a system that was linear, causal (the response comes only from the excitation), stable (unchanged during the sweep) and finite. For such a system the real and imaginary parts are tied together by the Kramers–Kronig relations: one can be computed from the other."],[21,"p","f11-14","figure L3.7","The relations need every frequency from zero to infinity, which no measurement has, so in practice a test circuit is fitted instead: many R‖C elements with fixed, evenly spread time constants, only their resistances free. Any valid spectrum can be fitted this way; if the fit fails, something is wrong with the data. The residuals should be small and scattered randomly around zero, well below 1 %. The test is used in only a minority of published work on commercial cells, though more often now that DRT, which needs excellent data, has spread."],[21,"p","f11-14","figure L3.7","Check the measurement before believing the model: rest, temperature, connection, amplitude, then the Kramers–Kronig test."],[22,"p","","","What does a spectrum mean, and what does a good fit not prove?"],[22,"f","f11-15","Figure L4.1","Two circuits, one spectrum: a perfect fit proves nothing about the circuit. Try this: change R2 and watch the ladder’s values change while the two curves stay on top of each other."],[22,"s","s-from-spectrum-to-meaning","From spectrum to meaning",""],[22,"p","s-from-spectrum-to-meaning","From spectrum to meaning","With a valid spectrum in hand, we can ask what it means. The usual analysis fits an equivalent circuit to the spectrum by complex nonlinear least squares and judges the fit by its χ2. The trap is that circuits are not unique: different circuits can give exactly the same impedance at every frequency, and adding elements always improves the fit. The same model can also return different values depending on the starting guesses and the weighting."],[22,"p","s-from-spectrum-to-meaning","From spectrum to meaning","So the circuit has to come from the physics of the cell, each element tied to a process, with as few elements as possible. Physics-based models go further: the transmission line of figure L3.4 with spherical diffusion in each grain, or full electrochemical models solved for their impedance."],[22,"f","f11-16","Figure L4.2","The distribution of relaxation times: overlapping arcs become separate peaks, if the regularization is right. Try this: find the range of λ that gives exactly two peaks; then go one step either side."],[22,"p","f11-16","figure L4.2","If choosing the circuit is the problem, we can try not to choose one in advance. That is what the distribution of relaxation times (DRT) does. Every R‖C pair relaxes with one time constant, so a spectrum can be written as a sum, in the limit a distribution, of such pairs:"],[22,"p","f11-16","figure L4.2","Recovering g(τ) turns the spectrum into peaks along the time axis, one per process, even where the arcs overlap in a Nyquist plot. That is why DRT is used on commercial cells: to count the processes and then build a circuit with the right number of elements."],[22,"p","f11-16","figure L4.2","The catch is that recovering g from noisy data is unstable and needs a regularization, a penalty of strength λ ; only a narrow range of λ gives the right answer, and the number of peaks depends on it. Bad data points distort it strongly, so DRT needs a Kramers–Kronig check first, and because the inductive and diffusion parts distort it too, it is usually applied to the SEI and charge-transfer arcs."],[22,"f","f11-17","Figure L4.3","Following a cell as it ages, and the data-driven route. Try this: play the ageing and compare how fast the interface resistance and the ohmic resistance grow."],[22,"s","s-following-ageing","Following ageing",""],[22,"p","s-following-ageing","Following ageing","We now have the tools to do what chapter 16 asked of us: follow a cell as it ages. Impedance generally rises as a cell ages, and because the measurement does not harm the cell, it can be repeated through its whole life. Ageing studies run check-ups: the same measurement at fixed conditions every so many cycles, often enough to follow the ageing, rarely enough not to add to it. Ageing has two visible effects: capacity fade, from losing active material, and power fade, from growing resistance through lost contacts, thickening surface films and delamination."],[22,"p","s-following-ageing","Following ageing","A spectrum is reduced to a few features: fitted resistances, DRT peaks or impedance at chosen frequencies. Individual contributions may follow the state of health, and give early warning of sudden fade, better than the total impedance. The difficulty is that temperature, state of charge and ageing all move the same spectrum; for some chemistries, LiFePO4 in particular, estimating the state of charge from impedance is much harder than for NMC."],[22,"p","s-following-ageing","Following ageing","Machine learning is the newest route: train a model on features from many cells, then predict the state of health or charge of a new one. Gaussian process regression is common, and models based on relaxation times have reached errors around 1 %. Their knowledge ends at their training data, and the studies so far cover few systems and conditions. Local impedance, measured on one spot of an electrode, is a further step, since a whole-cell spectrum averages over a structure that is not uniform."],[23,"p","","","When the outside of a cell is not enough, what then?"],[23,"f","f11-18","Figure L5.1","What each technique sees, and what it costs the cell. Try this: pick one thief from chapter 16 and find the cheapest column that could catch it."],[23,"s","s-when-the-outside-is-not-enough","When the outside is not enough",""],[23,"p","s-when-the-outside-is-not-enough","When the outside is not enough","Everything so far was measured from outside, across both electrodes at once, and that is its limit. Telling the two electrodes apart needs a reference electrode inside the cell, placed where it does not disturb the current, which is still hard to build and not a production solution. The alternative is to open the cell. Before that come non-destructive checks: inspection, X-ray tomography, capacity, cyclic voltammetry and differential capacity. Then the cell is discharged slowly and opened in an argon glove box; its electrodes can be rebuilt into half cells or symmetric cells of two identical electrodes, whose spectra show which electrode carries which arc, and examined by microscopy and spectroscopy. A fresh cell treated the same way is the baseline."],[23,"p","s-when-the-outside-is-not-enough","When the outside is not enough","The full picture often needs this tear-down, with Raman spectroscopy, atomic force microscopy, NMR, electron microscopy and X-ray absorption, while accelerating-rate calorimetry finds the conditions for thermal runaway. Plastic lithium-ion cells opened the way to watching the electrodes while they work, and an external pressure sensor can already follow the SEI growing through the swelling it causes."],[23,"p","s-when-the-outside-is-not-enough","When the outside is not enough","The cell itself has to be chosen too. The standard way to test a new positive electrode is a half-cell against lithium metal, but half-cells can fail to predict how the same electrode behaves in a full cell, and small assembly errors change the result; in GITT, the lithium counter electrode’s own overvoltage can pass for slow diffusion."],[23,"p","s-when-the-outside-is-not-enough","When the outside is not enough","You can now explain why a pulse resistance needs its timescale; how GITT turns two voltage changes into a diffusion coefficient, and the four checks that make it trustworthy (pulse length, IR drop, rest, particle size); what impedance is, and how to read Nyquist and Bode plots of a real cell from left to right; why the measurement must be small, steady, four-terminal and Kramers–Kronig valid; why a good circuit fit is not proof; and what DRT, nonlinear impedance and machine learning add."],[23,"p","s-when-the-outside-is-not-enough","When the outside is not enough","We can now find out from outside what has gone wrong in a cell, and open it when the outside is not enough. The last module takes up what is left: how to keep the energy a cell stores from coming out all at once, and what becomes of the cell when it is truly finished."]]};
